To prevent the lines from being fetched before the `fetch` request is complete, you can add the lines inside the `then` block of the `fetch` request. This ensures that the lines are only executed after the `fetch` request is completed and the data has been received. 

For example:

```
fetch('example.com').then(response => {
  // Code to be executed once data is received
  // This is where you can add the lines to be executed
  // after the fetch request is complete
  console.log('Data received:', response);
  // ...
}).catch(error => {
  // Handle any errors that may occur during the fetch request
  console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
});
```

By doing this, the lines you want to execute after the fetch request will only run once the data has been received, and not before.

To ensure that the code does not run until the `fetch` request is complete, you can use the `async/await` syntax. This allows you to write asynchronous code in a synchronous style, which can simplify your code and make it easier to understand.

Here's an example:

```js
async function getData() {
  const response = await fetch('https://example.com/api/data');
  const data = await response.json();

  // Code that depends on the `data`
  console.log(data);
}

getData(); // This will only execute after the `fetch` request is complete
```

In this example, we define an asynchronous function called `getData`. Inside the function, we use `await` to pause the execution until the `fetch` request and the JSON parsing have completed. This means that the code that depends on the data will only be executed once the data is available.

Then, we call the `getData` function, which will only execute after the `fetch` request is complete. This ensures that the code does not run until the fetch is complete and the data is available.

Note that in order to use `async/await`, the function has to be defined with the `async` keyword, and the `await` keyword can only be used inside an `async` function.

