a) To implement a UDP echo server in Java, we will create a DatagramSocket, bind it to a specific port, and listen for incoming packets. After receiving a packet, the server will send back an echo message to the client and then clear the buffer to prevent buffer overflow attacks. Below is an example of the Java code for a UDP echo server:

```java
import java.net.*;
import java.io.IOException;

public class UDPEchoServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Bind server socket to a specific port
        int port = 1234; // Specify the port number
        DatagramSocket serverSocket = new DatagramSocket(port);

        // Declare buffers and packets
        byte[] receiveData = new byte[1024];
        byte[] sendData;

        while (true) {
            DatagramPacket receivePacket = new DatagramPacket(receiveData, receiveData.length);

            // Receive the incoming packet
            serverSocket.receive(receivePacket);

            // Get information about the received packet
            InetAddress clientIP = receivePacket.getAddress();
            int clientPort = receivePacket.getPort();
            String receivedMessage = new String(receivePacket.getData(), 0, receivePacket.getLength());

            // Send back the echo message
            sendData = receivedMessage.getBytes();
            DatagramPacket sendPacket = new DatagramPacket(sendData, sendData.length, clientIP, clientPort);
            serverSocket.send(sendPacket);

            // Clear the buffer after sending the message
            for (int i = 0; i < receiveData.length; i++) {
                receiveData[i] = 0;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

b) To convert the server message broadcast program into a server message multicast program, we will use a MulticastSocket instead of a regular DatagramSocket. MulticastSocket allows sending packets to multiple clients that joined a specific multicast address. Below is an example of how to create a multicast server in Java:

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.*;

public class UDPMulticastServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Specify the multicast address and port
        InetAddress multicastAddress = InetAddress.getByName("230.0.0.1");
        int port = 1234;

        // Create a multicast socket
        MulticastSocket multicastSocket = new MulticastSocket();

        // Prepare the message to be sent
        String message = "Hello, multicast clients!";
        byte[] sendData = message.getBytes();

        // Send the multicast message to the clients
        DatagramPacket sendPacket = new DatagramPacket(sendData, sendData.length, multicastAddress, port);
        multicastSocket.send(sendPacket);

        // Close the multicast socket
        multicastSocket.close();
    }
}
```

In this example, the server sends a multicast message to clients that joined the multicast address "230.0.0.1" on port 1234. Clients can join this multicast group using the joinGroup() method of the MulticastSocket class.

