The implementation of the Separating Axis Theorem can vary depending on the programming language and the needs of your project. However, here is a simple example of SAT collision detection in JavaScript:

```javascript
function testSAT(polygonA, polygonB) {
    // loop over the edges of both polygons (including the edges between first and last points)
    for (let edge of polygonA.edges.concat(polygonB.edges)) {
        // get the normal vector for this edge (perpendicular to the edge, and pointing outwards)
        let axis = edge.normal();

        // project the vertices of both polygons onto the axis
        let projA = polygonA.project(axis);
        let projB = polygonB.project(axis);

        // check if the projections overlap
        if (!projA.overlap(projB)) {
            // if there is a separation on this axis, the polygons do not intersect
            return false;
        }
    }
    // if there is no separation on any axis, the polygons intersect
    return true;
}
```

In this code, `polygonA` and `polygonB` are objects that represent the two polygons you want to test for intersection. They should have `edges` properties that contain arrays of vectors representing the edges of the polygons.

The `edge.normal()` method returns a vector perpendicular to the edge and facing outwards, which is used as the axis for projection. The `polygonA.project(axis)` and `polygonB.project(axis)` methods project the vertices of the polygons onto the axis, returning an object with `min` and `max` properties representing the min and max values of the projection.

The `projA.overlap(projB)` method checks if the projections overlap or not. If there is no overlap on any axis, the polygons do not intersect and `false` is returned. Otherwise, if there is no separation on any axis, the polygons intersect and `true` is returned.

Note that this implementation does not calculate the direction or depth of overlap, which may be necessary in determining how to resolve the collision. Additionally, it assumes that the polygons are convex, and may not work as expected with concave or self-intersecting shapes.

