To use JavaScript and IndexedDB to store data in your website and blog, you can follow these steps:

1. First, you need to open a database. You can use the `indexedDB.open()` method to create a new database or open an existing one. 

```javascript
const dbName = 'myDatabase';
const storeName = 'myStore';

const request = indexedDB.open(dbName, 1);

request.onerror = function(event) {
    console.error('Error opening IndexedDB:', event.target.errorCode);
};

request.onupgradeneeded = function(event) {
    const db = event.target.result;
    const store = db.createObjectStore(storeName, { keyPath: 'id', autoIncrement: true });
    store.createIndex('title', 'title', { unique: false });
    store.createIndex('content', 'content', { unique: false });
};
```

2. To save data in the database, use the `add` method:

```javascript
function saveData(title, content) {
    const request = indexedDB.open(dbName);

    request.onsuccess = function(event) {
        const db = event.target.result;

        const transaction = db.transaction(storeName, 'readwrite');
        const store = transaction.objectStore(storeName);
        store.add({ title: title, content: content });

        transaction.onerror = function(event) {
            console.error('Error saving data:', event.target.errorCode);
        };

        transaction.oncomplete = function() {
            console.log('Data saved successfully');
            db.close();
        };
    };
}
```

3. To retrieve data from the database, use the `getAll` method:

```javascript
function getData() {
    const request = indexedDB.open(dbName);

    request.onsuccess = function(event) {
        const db = event.target.result;

        const transaction = db.transaction(storeName, 'readonly');
        const store = transaction.objectStore(storeName);
        const getAllRequest = store.getAll();

        getAllRequest.onsuccess = function(event) {
            console.log('All data:', getAllRequest.result);
            db.close();
        };
    };
}
```

4. To delete data from the database, use the `delete` method:

```javascript
function deleteData(id) {
    const request = indexedDB.open(dbName);

    request.onsuccess = function(event) {
        const db = event.target.result;
        const transaction = db.transaction(storeName, 'readwrite');
        const store = transaction.objectStore(storeName);
        store.delete(id);

        transaction.onerror = function(event) {
            console.error('Error deleting data:', event.target.errorCode);
        };

        transaction.oncomplete = function() {
            console.log('Data deleted successfully');
            db.close();
        };
    };
}
```

Now you can use these functions to store and manage your website and blog data. Just replace the variables `title` and `content` with the appropriate data from your blog or website.

A Single Page Application (SPA) router enables navigation in your application without having to reload the page. Here's a simple SPA router using JavaScript:

1. First, create a basic HTML file with a `div` element that serves as the container for rendering your views:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My SPA</title>
</head>
<body>
    <div id="root"></div>
    <script src="spa-router.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Create a `spa-router.js` file that contains your routing configuration and the logic for managing routes, rendering views, and handling route changes:

```javascript
// Define routes.
const routes = [
    {
        path: '/',
        view: () => 'Home Page',
    },
    {
        path: '/about',
        view: () => 'About Page',
    },
    {
        path: '/contact',
        view: () => 'Contact Page',
    },
    {
        path: '*',
        view: () => '404 - Page Not Found',
    },
];

// Get the current path.
function getCurrentPath() {
    return window.location.pathname || '/';
}

// Find the route corresponding to the current path.
function findRouteByPath(path) {
    return (
        routes.find((route) => route.path === path) ||
        routes.find((route) => route.path === '*')
    );
}

// Render the view associated with the route.
function renderView(route) {
    const root = document.getElementById('root');
    if (!root) return;

    root.innerHTML = route.view();
}

// Set up a simple router that handles navigation.
function setupRouter() {
    window.addEventListener('popstate', () => {
        const currentPath = getCurrentPath();
        const currentRoute = findRouteByPath(currentPath);
        renderView(currentRoute);
    });
}

// Initialize the router and render the view for the first time.
function initRouter() {
    setupRouter();
    const currentPath = getCurrentPath();
    const currentRoute = findRouteByPath(currentPath);
    renderView(currentRoute);
}

// Start the router.
initRouter();
```

Now, you have a basic SPA router in place. It uses the browser history API to handle navigation without page reloads. To add new routes, simply add new route objects to the `routes` array and define their views.

To update your navigation links, you can use the following method:

```javascript
function navigateTo(path) {
    window.history.pushState(null, '', path);
    const route = findRouteByPath(path);
    renderView(route);
}
```

In your HTML, use the `navigateTo()` method for navigation:

```html
<a href="javascript:void(0);" onclick="navigateTo('/')">Home</a>
<a href="javascript:void(0);" onclick="navigateTo('/about')">About</a>
<a href="javascript:void(0);" onclick="navigateTo('/contact')">Contact</a>
```

Now, you have a simple SPA router set up using JavaScript.

To expand upon the SPA router, you can integrate dynamic routes, create view components, and handle more complex route configurations. Here's an example of how to create a more advanced SPA router:

1. Update the `routes` array to include a dynamic route with a URL parameter (e.g., '/post/:id'):

```javascript
const routes = [
    //... other routes
    {
        path: '/post/:id',
        view: ({ id }) => `Post Page: ${id}`,
    },
];
```

2. Modify the `findRouteByPath()` function to support URL parameters:

```javascript
function findRouteByPath(path) {
    for (const route of routes) {
        const paramKeys = [];
        const pattern = route.path
            .replace(/([:*])(\w+)/g, (_, type, key) => {
                paramKeys.push(key);
                return type === ':' ? '([^/]+)' : '(.+)';
            })
            .concat('/?');
        const regex = new RegExp(`^${pattern}$`, 'i');
        const paramValues = path.match(regex);
        if (paramValues) {
            const params = paramKeys.reduce(
                (acc, key, index) => ({ ...acc, [key]: paramValues[index + 1] }),
                {}
            );
            return { ...route, params };
        }
    }
    return routes.find((route) => route.path === '*');
}
```

3. Update the `renderView()` function to pass route parameters to the view:

```javascript
function renderView(route) {
    const root = document.getElementById('root');
    if (!root) return;

    root.innerHTML = route.view(route.params || {});
}
```

4. Create a new function `createLink()` to simplify creating navigation links:

```javascript
function createLink(path, text) {
    const link = document.createElement('a');
    link.href = 'javascript:void(0);';
    link.onclick = () => navigateTo(path);
    link.textContent = text;
    return link;
}
```

5. Use the `createLink()` function to dynamically generate navigation links in your HTML:

```javascript
const nav = document.getElementById('nav');
nav.appendChild(createLink('/', 'Home'));
nav.appendChild(createLink('/about', 'About'));
nav.appendChild(createLink('/contact', 'Contact'));
```

6. Replace the view functions in the `routes` array with functions that return HTML elements:

```javascript
const routes = [
    {
        path: '/',
        view: () => {
            const container = document.createElement('div');
            container.textContent = 'Home Page';
            return container;
        },
    },
    {
        path: '/about',
        view: () => {
            const container = document.createElement('div');
            container.textContent = 'About Page';
            return container;
        },
    },
    {
        path: '/contact',
        view: () => {
            const container = document.createElement('div');
            container.textContent = 'Contact Page';
            return container;
        },
    },
    {
        path: '/post/:id',
        view: ({ id }) => {
            const container = document.createElement('div');
            container.textContent = `Post Page: ${id}`;
            return container;
        },
    },
    {
        path: '*',
        view: () => {
            const container = document.createElement('div');
            container.textContent = '404 - Page Not Found';
            return container;
        },
    },
];
```

7. Modify the `renderView()` function to append an HTML element instead of setting `innerHTML`:

```javascript
function renderView(route) {
    const root = document.getElementById('root');
    if (!root) return;

    while (root.firstChild) {
        root.removeChild(root.firstChild);
    }
    root.appendChild(route.view(route.params || {}));
}
```

Now, you have an advanced SPA router that supports dynamic routes, URL parameters, and view components. With this router, you can build a more complex application that can handle various use cases, such as rendering blog posts, displaying user profiles, or even showing different types of content based on URL parameters.

Here's how to further advance your SPA router by adding nested routes and asynchronously loading view components:

1. Introduce nested routes for the existing routes:

```javascript
const routes = [
    {
        path: '/',
        view: () => createViewComponent('Home Page'),
        children: [
            {
                path: 'subpage',
                view: () => createViewComponent('Home Subpage'),
            },
        ],
    },
    {
        path: '/about',
        view: () => createViewComponent('About Page'),
    },
    // ... other routes
];
```

2. Update the `findRouteByPath()` function to support nested routes:

```javascript
function findRouteByPath(path) {
    let remainingPath = path;
    let currentRoutes = routes;

    const routeStack = [];

    while (remainingPath || currentRoutes) {
        const currentRoute =
            currentRoutes.find((route) => {
                const { params, remaining } = matchRoutePath(route.path, remainingPath);
                if (remaining !== undefined) {
                    remainingPath = remaining;
                    routeStack.push({ ...route, params });
                    return true;
                }
                return false;
            }) || {};

        if (currentRoute.children) {
            currentRoutes = currentRoute.children;
        } else {
            break;
        }
    }

    return routeStack.length > 0 ? routeStack : [{ ...findRouteByPath('*') }];
}
```

3. Create a helper function `matchRoutePath()` to match a route:

```javascript
function matchRoutePath(pattern, path) {
    const keys = [];
    const regex = new RegExp(`^${pattern.replace(/:([a-z]+)/gi, (_, key) => {
        keys.push(key);
        return '([^/]+)';
    })}`, 'i');
    const matches = path.match(regex);

    if (matches) {
        const params = keys.reduce(
            (acc, key, index) => ({ ...acc, [key]: matches[index + 1] }),
            {}
        );
        const remaining = path.replace(regex, '');
        return { params, remaining };
    }

    return {};
}
```

4. Modify the `renderView()` function to support nested views:

```javascript
function renderView(routeStack, parent) {
    const { view, params, children } = routeStack.shift() || {};

    if (!view || !parent) {
        return;
    }

    const component = view(params || {});
    parent.innerHTML = '';
    parent.appendChild(component);

    if (routeStack.length > 0) {
        renderView(routeStack, component.querySelector('.child-view'));
    }
}
```

5. Update the `createViewComponent()` function to create a container for nested views:

```javascript
function createViewComponent(content) {
    const container = document.createElement('div');
    container.innerHTML = `<h1>${content}</h1><div class="child-view"></div>`;
    return container;
}
```

6. Change the `initRouter()` function to use a route stack:

```javascript
function initRouter() {
    setupRouter();
    const currentPath = getCurrentPath();
    const routeStack = findRouteByPath(currentPath);
    renderView(routeStack, document.getElementById('root'));
}
```

7. Now, let's support asynchronous loading of components to increase performance and reduce the initial load time. Update routes to use `import()` to load components:

```javascript
const Home = () => import(/* webpackChunkName: "home" */ './components/Home');
const About = () => import(/* webpackChunkName: "about" */ './components/About');
const Contact = () => import(/* webpackChunkName: "contact" */ './components/Contact');
const Post = () => import(/* webpackChunkName: "post" */ './components/Post');
const NotFound = () => import(/* webpackChunkName: "not-found" */ './components/NotFound');

const routes = [
    {
        path: '/',
        view: Home,
    },
    {
        path: '/about',
        view: About,
    },
    {
        path: '/contact',
        view: Contact,
    },
    {
        path: '/post/:id',
        view: Post,
    },
    {
        path: '*',
        view: NotFound,
    },
];
```

Here, each route has a view that returns a Promise that resolves to a component. Update the `renderView()` function to handle asynchronous components:

```javascript
async function renderView(routeStack, parent) {
    const { view, params, children } = routeStack.shift() || {};

    if (!view || !parent) {
        return;
    }

    const component = await view(params || {}).then((comp) => comp.default(params));
    parent.innerHTML = '';
    parent.appendChild(component);

    if (routeStack.length > 0) {
        renderView(routeStack, component.querySelector('.child-view'));
    }
}
```

Now, you've advanced the SPA router to:

- Support nested routes via route stack rendering.
- Asynchronously load components, reducing initial loading time and increasing performance.
- Create a container for nested views using `createViewComponent()`.

These enhancements allow you to create a modular, maintainable, and high-performance SPA.

Apologies for the confusion; the `import()` function is not explicitly defined in the previous examples. 

`import()` is a built-in JavaScript feature (Dynamic Imports) that allows you to load modules asynchronously. It returns a Promise that resolves to the module when the module is fetched and executed. You can use dynamic imports to separate parts of your application into separate chunks, reducing the initial loading time and improving performance.

Here's a simple example of how to use `import()` to load a module:

1. Create a file `example-module.js`:

```javascript
// example-module.js
export function greet() {
    console.log('Hello from the example module!');
}
```

2. Use `import()` to dynamically import and use the module in your application:

```javascript
// main.js
document.getElementById('load-module').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const { greet } = await import('./example-module.js');
    greet();
});
```

3. Create a simple HTML file:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Dynamic Import Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <button id="load-module">Load Module</button>
    <script src="main.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

Now, when clicking the "Load Module" button, the `example-module.js` will be fetched and executed, displaying "Hello from the example module!" in the console.

So, the `import()` function is a built-in JavaScript feature, and you can use it directly in your code to enable dynamic imports. Note that depending on your project configuration (e.g., using webpack or another bundler), you may have to adjust your setup to support dynamic imports properly.

