Suppose we are given a system of linear equations:

5x + y - z = 10

x + 6y + 2z = -4

3x - y + 10z = 12

We can represent this as a matrix equation A*x = f, where

A = [5 1 -1; 1 6 2; 3 -1 10]

x = [x; y; z]

f = [10; -4; 12]

To solve for x, we can use the chase function as follows:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int chase(double a[], double b[], double c[], double f[], double yy[], int n);

int main()
{
    int i, n = 3;
    double a[n], b[n], c[n], f[n], x[n], yy[n];

    // Set the matrix and vector values
    a[0] = 0; b[0] = 5; c[0] = 1; f[0] = 10;
    a[1] = 1; b[1] = 6; c[1] = 2; f[1] = -4;
    a[2] = -1; b[2] = -1; c[2] = 10; f[2] = 12;

    // Call the chase function to solve for x
    chase(a, b, c, f, yy, n);

    // Back-substitution to find x
    x[n-1] = yy[n-1];
    for (i = n-2; i >= 0; i--)
    {
        x[i] = yy[i] - c[i]*x[i+1];
    }

    // Print the solution
    printf("The solution is:\n");
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        printf("x[%d] = %f\n", i, x[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

