Here's an updated version of the `velocityToPosition` method that should correctly handle overshooting:

```java
public static Vector2 velocityToPosition(Body body, Vector2 targetPosition, float maxSpeed) {
    Vector2 currentPosition = body.getPosition();
    Vector2 currentVelocity = body.getLinearVelocity();

    // Calculate the distance to the target position
    Vector2 targetDirection = targetPosition.cpy().sub(currentPosition);
    float distanceToTarget = targetDirection.len();

    // Calculate the maximum distance that can be covered in one time step
    float maxDistance = maxSpeed / PhysicsSystem.STEP_FREQUENCY;

    // Calculate the time needed to reach the target without overshooting
    float timeToTarget = Math.max(0, distanceToTarget - maxDistance) / maxSpeed;

    // Calculate the velocity needed to reach the target without overshooting
    Vector2 targetVelocity = targetDirection.scl(1f / timeToTarget);

    // Apply a proportional gain to blend the current velocity with the target velocity
    float proportionalGain = 0.2f; // adjust as needed
    Vector2 blendedVelocity = currentVelocity.cpy().scl(1f - proportionalGain).add(targetVelocity.scl(proportionalGain));

    // Clamp the velocity to the maximum speed
    float blendedSpeed = blendedVelocity.len();
    if (blendedSpeed > maxSpeed) {
        blendedVelocity.scl(maxSpeed / blendedSpeed);
    }

    return blendedVelocity;
}
```

This implementation calculates the distance to the target position and the maximum distance that can be covered in one time step, similar to the previous version. However, it then calculates the time needed to reach the target position without overshooting. This time is used to calculate the velocity needed to reach the target position without overshooting, which is blended with the current velocity of the body using a proportional gain. Finally, the resulting velocity is clamped to the maximum speed.

Note that the proportional gain parameter may need to be adjusted based on the specifics of your simulation. A higher value will result in the body approaching the target position more quickly, but may also increase the risk of overshooting.

