To create the desired API, you can follow these steps:

Step 1: Install Laravel and MongoDB package
1. As you are using Laravel, install it first by running the following command:
```
composer create-project --prefer-dist laravel/laravel your-project-name
```
2. Now, install the jenssegers/mongodb package:
```
composer require jenssegers/mongodb
```

Step 2: Configure MongoDB
1. Configure your `.env` file with these settings to enable MongoDB connection:
```sh
DB_CONNECTION=mongodb
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=27017
DB_DATABASE=your_database_name
DB_USERNAME=
DB_PASSWORD=
```

2. Open `config/app.php` to register the MongoDB service provider, add the following provider to the `providers` array:
```sh
Jenssegers\Mongodb\MongodbServiceProvider::class,
```

Step 3: Create a model for the document
1. Create a model for your document using `php artisan make:model Document`
2. Add the connection and collection on your newly created `Document` model like this:

```php
<?php

namespace App;

use Jenssegers\Mongodb\Eloquent\Model;

class Document extends Model
{
    protected $connection = 'mongodb';
    protected $collection = 'documents';
}
```
3. Add necessary fields as required.

Step 4: Create the API route and controller
1. Create the API controller using `php artisan make:controller Api/DocumentController`
2. Within the `routes/api.php` file, add the route to your controller:
```php
Route::post('documents', 'Api\DocumentController@store');
```
3. Now, update the `DocumentController.php` `store` method:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers\Api;

use App\Http\Controllers\Controller;
use App\Document;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class DocumentController extends Controller
{
    public function store(Request $request)
    {
        // Save data to MongoDB
        $data = $request->all();
        $document = new Document($data);
        $document->save();

        // Log the request
        $this->logRequest($request);

        return response()->json(['message' => 'Document saved successfully']);
    }
}
```

Step 5: Create a middleware for logging and gzip
1. Create a middleware using `php artisan make:middleware LogRequest`
2. Insert the following code:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Carbon\Carbon;

class LogRequest
{
    public function handle($request, Closure $next)
    {
        $response = $next($request);

        // Log the request
        $this->logRequest($request);

        return $response;
    }

    private function logRequest($request)
    {
        $logFile = storage_path('logs/requests/' . Carbon::now()->format('Y-m-d') . '.log');
        $logDir = dirname($logFile);
        if (!file_exists($logDir)) {
            mkdir($logDir, 0755, true);
        }
        $logMessage = '[' . Carbon::now()->toDateTimeString() . ']';
        $logMessage .= ' ' . $request->method();
        $logMessage .= ' ' . $request->url();
        $logMessage .= ' ' . $request->ip();
        $logMessage .= PHP_EOL . json_encode($request->all()) . PHP_EOL . PHP_EOL;

        file_put_contents($logFile, $logMessage, FILE_APPEND);
    }
}
```

3. Now, register the middleware in `app/Http/Kernel.php` by adding this to the `routeMiddleware` array:
```php
'log_request' => \App\Http\Middleware\LogRequest::class,
```

4. In `routes/api.php` file, use the middleware with the route you created:
```php
Route::post('documents', 'Api\DocumentController@store')->middleware('log_request');
```

Step 6: Schedule compression of log files
1. Configure Laravel scheduler by modifying the `app/Console/Kernel.php` file:

```php
protected function schedule(Schedule $schedule)
{
    $schedule->command('compress:logs')->daily();
}
```

2. Create the command using `php artisan make:command CompressLogs`
3. Edit the `CompressLogs.php` file:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Console\Commands;

use Illuminate\Console\Command;
use Carbon\Carbon;
use RecursiveDirectoryIterator;
use RecursiveIteratorIterator;
use ZipArchive;

class CompressLogs extends Command
{
    protected $signature = 'compress:logs';
    protected $description = 'Compress daily logs to a gz file';

    public function __construct()
    {
        parent::__construct();
    }

    public function handle()
    {
        $logDir = storage_path('logs/requests');
        $logFiles = new RecursiveIteratorIterator(new RecursiveDirectoryIterator($logDir));
        $date = Carbon::yesterday()->format('Y-m-d');

        foreach ($logFiles as $logFile) {
            if ($logFile->isFile() && $logFile->getFilename() == $date . '.log') {
                $gzFile = $logDir . '/' . $date . '.gz';
                $this->compress($logFile->getPathname(), $gzFile);

                // Remove original log file after compression
                unlink($logFile->getPathname());

                $this->info('Compressed ' . $logFile->getPathname());
            }
        }
    }

    private function compress($source, $destination)
    {
        $handle = gzopen($destination, 'wb9');
        gzwrite($handle, file_get_contents($source));
        gzclose($handle);
    }
}
```

This entire setup will create an API to store documents in MongoDB and log requests. The requests will be gzipped daily as per the scheduled command.

I’m building a video game engine using C++ as the coding language and Vulkan for graphics. I am trying to set up a generic renderer using Vulkan that is flexible and will render objects based on a vector that is supplied to it. The renderer will also handle the creation of the window using GLFW and use GLM for all relevant math calls.

I have written the code for the foundations of the engine.

The basic class structure is as follows:

1. Core:

- class Engine
- This is the main class that will initialize, run, and shut down other subsystems of the engine (e.g., Window, Renderer, Input, etc.)

2. Window:

- class Window
- This class will handle window creation, resizing, and destruction using GLFW. It should also handle keyboard and mouse input events.

3. Renderer:

- class Renderer
