Bidirectional-streams Over Synchronous HTTP (BOSH) is a transport protocol that emulates a bidirectional stream between two entities (such as a client and server) by using multiple synchronous HTTP request-response pairs without requiring the use of polling or asynchronous chunking. To create a BOSH implementation in C, you can use the `libcurl` library to issue HTTP requests and handle the responses. 

Please note that BOSH is typically used in the context of the Extensible Messaging and Presence Protocol (XMPP). Here is an example of a minimal C program that demonstrates a BOSH connection setup:

Setup `libcurl` library by installing it. For Ubuntu, use the following command:
```
sudo apt-get install libcurl4-gnutls-dev
```

Compile the code using the command:
```
gcc bosh_example.c -o bosh_example -lcurl
```

bosh_example.c:
```c
#include <curl/curl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

static size_t write_callback(void *contents, size_t size, size_t nmemb, void *userp) {
  size_t realsize = size * nmemb;
  if (realsize > 0) {
    printf("Received data: %s\n", (char *)contents);
  }
  return realsize;
}

int main() {
  CURL *curl;
  CURLcode res;
  struct curl_slist *headers = NULL;

  curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_DEFAULT);
  curl = curl_easy_init();

  if (!curl) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Error initializing libcurl\n");
    return 1;
  }

  char *bosh_url = "https://your-bosh-service.example.com/http-bind/";
  char *xml_body = "<body content='text/xml; charset=utf-8' "
                   "xmlns='http://jabber.org/protocol/httpbind' "
                   "xmlns:xmpp='urn:xmpp:xbosh' "
                   "xmpp:version='1.0'></body>";

  headers = curl_slist_append(headers, "Content-Type: text/xml; charset=utf-8");

  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, bosh_url);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER, headers);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, xml_body);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, write_callback);

  res = curl_easy_perform(curl);

  // Check for errors.
  if (res != CURLE_OK) {
    fprintf(stderr, "curl_easy_perform() failed: %s\n", curl_easy_strerror(res));
  }

  // Clean up.
  curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
  curl_slist_free_all(headers);

  curl_global_cleanup();
  return 0;
}
```

This example demonstrates a minimal BOSH connection setup using the `libcurl` library to send an HTTP POST request with a sample BOSH payload to a BOSH service. The response payload is received and printed in the write_callback function. Replace `your-bosh-service.example.com` with the actual BOSH service URL.

Please note that this example only demonstrates a single HTTP request-response pair, while BOSH uses multiple pairs to maintain the bidirectional stream. You would need to implement the proper BOSH state machine to manage and maintain the BOSH connection, handle retries, and other connection details according to the BOSH/XMPP specifications.

An HTTP tunnel using the POST method can be implemented using libcurl in C. Here's an example of a basic HTTP tunnel setup that sends and receives data using POST requests.

1. Install the libcurl library:

For Ubuntu, use the following command:
```sh
sudo apt-get install libcurl4-gnutls-dev
```

2. Compile the code using the following command:
```sh
gcc http_tunnel_example.c -o http_tunnel_example -lcurl
```

3. C source code for http_tunnel_example.c:
```c
#include <curl/curl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

static size_t write_callback(void *contents, size_t size, size_t nmemb, void *userp) {
  size_t realsize = size * nmemb;
  if (realsize > 0) {
    printf("Received data from the server: %s\n", (char *)contents);
  }
  return realsize;
}

int main() {
  CURL *curl;
  CURLcode res;
  struct curl_slist *headers = NULL;

  curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_DEFAULT);
  curl = curl_easy_init();

  if (!curl) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Error initializing libcurl\n");
    return 1;
  }

  const char* tunnel_url = "https://your-server-url.example.com/tunnel";
  const char* post_data = "your data to send via HTTP POST";

  headers = curl_slist_append(headers, "Content-Type: text/plain");

  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, tunnel_url);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER, headers);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_POST, 1L);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, post_data);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDSIZE, (long)strlen(post_data));
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, write_callback);

  res = curl_easy_perform(curl);

  // Check for errors.
  if (res != CURLE_OK) {
    fprintf(stderr, "curl_easy_perform() failed: %s\n", curl_easy_strerror(res));
  }

  // Clean up.
  curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
