Ahora, cuando seleccionas un rango de fechas, los valores de `startDate` y `endDate` se actualizarán correctamente y se mostrarán en el `console.log` y se pasarán a la función `searchPending`.

To achieve this with the Ant Design Table, you can use the `rowSelection.getCheckboxProps` method provided for row selection.

First, you need to store the selected bank in the state. Then, on each row selection, if there are no rows selected, update the state with the new bank, or reset the state if no bank is selected. Finally, use `getCheckboxProps` to disable rows that don't match the selected bank.

Here's an example:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";
import { Table } from "antd";

const App = () => {
  const [selectedBank, setSelectedBank] = useState(null);
  const data = [
    // Your table data
  ];

  const columns = [
    // Your table columns
  ];

  const onSelectChange = (selectedRowKeys, selectedRows) => {
    if (selectedRows && selectedRows.length > 0) {
      setSelectedBank(selectedRows[0].bank);
    } else {
      setSelectedBank(null); // Reset the selected bank when no rows are selected
    }
  };

  const rowSelection = {
    onChange: onSelectChange,
    getCheckboxProps: record => ({
      disabled: selectedBank && selectedBank !== record.bank,
    }),
  };

  return (
    <>
      <Table
        rowSelection={rowSelection}
        columns={columns}
        dataSource={data}
      />
    </>
  );
};

export default App;
```

By doing this, you ensure that only rows with the same `bank` value can be selected. If any rows with different banks are present, they will be disabled as long as a row with a specific bank is selected right after the first selection.

