Here's a Java method that takes three points representing the vertices of a triangle and a point to check if it lies inside the triangle:

```java
public class TriangleCheck {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Test case
        Point a = new Point(0, 0);
        Point b = new Point(5, 0);
        Point c = new Point(0, 5);
        Point p = new Point(1, 1);

        System.out.println(isPointInTriangle(a, b, c, p)); // Output: true
    }

    public static boolean isPointInTriangle(Point a, Point b, Point c, Point p) {
        // Calculate area of the whole triangle
        double areaABC = calculateArea(a, b, c);

        // Calculate area of sub-triangles formed by the point p and vertices of the given triangle
        double areaABP = calculateArea(a, b, p);
        double areaBCP = calculateArea(b, c, p);
        double areaCAP = calculateArea(c, a, p);

        // Check if the sum of areas of sub-triangles is equal to the area of the whole triangle
        return Math.abs(areaABC - (areaABP + areaBCP + areaCAP)) < 1e-9;
    }

    public static double calculateArea(Point a, Point b, Point c) {
        return Math.abs((a.x * (b.y - c.y) + b.x * (c.y - a.y) + c.x * (a.y - b.y)) / 2.0);
    }

    static class Point {
        double x, y;

        public Point(double x, double y) {
            this.x = x;
            this.y = y;
        }
    }
}
```

This method first calculates the area of the triangle formed by the three input points (a, b, c) and then calculates the areas of three sub-triangles formed by including the point p. Finally, it checks if the sum of the areas of the three sub-triangles is equal to the area of the whole triangle. If it's true, it means that the point p lies inside the triangle.

