To implement a SharedViewModel in Jetpack Compose, follow these steps:

1. Define the ViewModel class that will be shared by multiple composables. This can be a regular ViewModel as defined in ViewModelProviders, but should extend AndroidViewModel for compatibility with the Android framework.

2. Define the shared state properties as MutableState variables in the ViewModel. These variables will store the current state of the UI. For example:

```kotlin
class SharedViewModel(application: Application) : AndroidViewModel(application) {
    val counter = mutableStateOf(0)
    val name = mutableStateOf("")
}
```

3. In each composable that needs access to the shared state, create a ViewModel instance using ViewModelProvider and retrieve the shared state variables from the ViewModel. For example:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyComposable() {
    val viewModel = viewModel<SharedViewModel>()
    val counter = viewModel.counter.value
    val name = viewModel.name.value

    // Update the state variables as needed
    viewModel.counter.value++
    viewModel.name.value = "New Name"
}
```

4. To ensure that the state variables are stored properly across configuration changes, use the rememberSaveable and stateFor functions to save and restore the state variables. For example:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyComposable() {
    val viewModel = viewModel<SharedViewModel>()
    val counter = viewModel.counter.value
    val name = viewModel.name.value
    val counterState = rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(counter) }
    val nameState = rememberSaveable { mutableStateOf(name) }

    // Update the state variables as needed
    counterState.value++
    nameState.value = "New Name"
    viewModel.counter.value = counterState.value
    viewModel.name.value = nameState.value

    // Use the state variables in the UI
    Text(text = "Counter: ${counterState.value}")
    TextField(value = nameState.value, onValueChange = { nameState.value = it })
}
``` 

By following these steps, you can effectively implement a SharedViewModel in Jetpack Compose, allowing multiple composables to access and update shared state variables from a central location.

If you don't want to store any value in the SharedViewModel, but want to ensure that it prevents resetting of other states, you can define a default state in the ViewModel and use that state in the reset function.

Here's an example implementation of a SharedViewModel without storing any value:

```kotlin
class SharedViewModel : ViewModel() {
    private val defaultState = "Default"

    private val _text = mutableStateOf(defaultState)
    val text: State<String> = _text

    fun onReset() {
        _text.value = defaultState
    }

    fun onUpdate(value: String) {
        _text.value = value
    }
}
```

In this example, the ViewModel has only one mutable state variable `_text` and does not store any value in it. Instead, it has a `defaultState` value that is used as the default state.

The `onReset()` function sets the `_text` value to the `defaultState`, effectively resetting the state to the default value.

The `onUpdate()` function allows updating the state value, but without storing it in the ViewModel. It directly sets the `_text` value to the updated value.

In the composables that use this ViewModel, you can call `onReset()` to reset the state to the default state, like so:

```kotlin
Column {
    Text(sharedViewModel.text.value)
    Button(onClick = { sharedViewModel.onReset() }) {
        Text("Reset")
    }
}
```

In this example, when the "Reset" button is clicked, it calls the `onReset()` function in the ViewModel, which resets the `_text` value to the default state. Other states that are stored in the ViewModel will not be affected by this reset.

