Yes, it is possible to store a class member function's pointer to a static vector variable for later usage in C++. Here is an example:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

class MyClass {
public:
    void myFunc() {
        std::cout << "Hello from myFunc!" << std::endl;
    }
};

class FunctionManager {
public:
    static std::vector<void (MyClass::*)()> functionList;

    static void addFunction(void (MyClass::*func)()) {
        functionList.push_back(func);
    }

    static void callFunctions(MyClass& obj) {
        for(auto func : functionList) {
            (obj.*func)();
        }
    }
};

std::vector<void (MyClass::*)()> FunctionManager::functionList;

int main() {
    MyClass obj;
    FunctionManager::addFunction(&MyClass::myFunc);
    FunctionManager::callFunctions(obj);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `FunctionManager` is a static class that manages a list of class member function pointers. The `addFunction` static function is used to add a function pointer to the list. The `callFunctions` static function is used to call all the functions in the list on a given `MyClass` object. The `MyClass` class has a simple function called `myFunc` that just prints a message to the console.

In `main`, we create an instance of `MyClass`, add `MyClass::myFunc` to the list of functions, and then call all the functions on `obj` using `FunctionManager::callFunctions`. This results in the message "Hello from myFunc!" being printed to the console.

