`std::vector` and `std::map` are not thread safe by default. This means that if multiple threads try to modify them concurrently without proper synchronization, it can lead to undefined behavior (such as a segmentation fault, or data corruption).

To make `std::vector` or `std::map` thread safe, you need to ensure that only one thread accesses them at a time. This can be achieved in a number of ways:

1. Use locks: Protect inserts, reads, erases, etc. with a mutex or other synchronization mechanism that prevents multiple threads from accessing the container at the same time.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>

std::vector<int> myVector;
std::mutex myMutex;

void addToVector(int value) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(myMutex);
    myVector.push_back(value);
}

int main() {
    addToVector(42);
    addToVector(7);
    addToVector(13);

    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(myMutex);
    for (auto const& value : myVector) {
        std::cout << value << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we use a `std::mutex` to ensure that only one thread can push elements onto the vector at a time. We use `std::lock_guard` to automatically acquire and release the lock, and we print the contents of the vector while holding the lock so that no other thread can modify the vector while we're reading from it.

2. Use atomic operations: If only one thread will be modifying the vector or map, you can use atomic operations to ensure that updates are visible to other threads.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <atomic>

std::vector<int> myVector;
std::atomic<int> myIndex(0);

void addToVector(int value) {
    int index = myIndex.fetch_add(1);
    myVector[index] = value;
}

int main() {
    myVector.resize(3);
    addToVector(42);
    addToVector(7);
    addToVector(13);

    for (auto const& value : myVector) {
        std::cout << value << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we use an `std::atomic<int>` to keep track of the current index in the vector, and use `fetch_add()` to safely increment the index. We assume in this example that only one thread will be modifying the vector and each element will be written to the vector by a different thread.

Note that some standard library implementations provide thread-safe versions of `std::vector` and `std::map` (e.g. `std::vector<std::atomic<T>>`, `std::shared_mutex`, `std::shared_lock`, etc.), so it's worth checking your implementation's documentation to see if they are available and suitable for your use case.

