To create an Android app that opens a TCP socket connection in a separate thread when started, you can follow the steps below:

Step 1: Set up a new Android project

1. Open Android Studio and create a new project with an empty activity.
2. Name your application, choose appropriate settings, and click Finish.
3. Add Internet permission to your AndroidManifest.xml file.

```xml
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.example.tcpsocketapp">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application ...>
        <activity ...>
           ...
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
```

Step 2: Create a SocketThread class

Create a new class called `SocketThread` that extends `Thread`. This class represents the separate thread that will open the TCP socket connection.

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.net.Socket;

public class SocketThread extends Thread {
    private Socket socket;
    private String serverIP;
    private int serverPort;

    public SocketThread(String serverIP, int serverPort) {
        this.serverIP = serverIP;
        this.serverPort = serverPort;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            socket = new Socket(serverIP, serverPort);
            PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true);
            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));

            // Process communication with the server here (send/receive messages)
            // ...

            out.close();
            in.close();
            socket.close();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    // Implement other methods (e.g., sending/receiving messages, etc.) as needed
}
```

Step 3: Start the SocketThread in MainActivity

In your `MainActivity.java`, start the `SocketThread` when the activity is created.

```java
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import android.os.Bundle;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private SocketThread socketThread;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

        String serverIP = "example.com"; // Replace with your server's IP address
        int serverPort = 12345; // Replace with your server's port number

        socketThread = new SocketThread(serverIP, serverPort);
        socketThread.start();
    }

    @Override
    protected void onDestroy() {
        super.onDestroy();
        // Close the socket connection when the activity is destroyed
        if (socketThread != null) {
            socketThread.interrupt();
        }
    }
}
```

Step 4: Build and run your app

Build and run your app on an Android device or emulator. When the app starts, it will open a TCP socket connection with the specified server address and port number in a separate thread.

**Note:** Make sure the specified serverIP and serverPort match with your actual TCP/IP server's settings. You may also need to handle exceptions or errors in a production app for better user experience.

To prevent the activity from restarting on screen rotation, you can add `android:configChanges` to your activity declaration in the AndroidManifest.xml file.

Here's how you can modify your AndroidManifest.xml file:

```xml
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.example.tcpsocketapp">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application ...>
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:configChanges="keyboardHidden|orientation|screenSize|screenLayout">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
```

With this change, the system will not restart your activity when the screen orientation changes, and the `onCreate()` method will not be called again. Instead, the system will call `onConfigurationChanged()` whenever there's a configuration change. If you need to handle configuration changes manually, you can override this method in your `MainActivity`. But, with the current implementation, you don't need to do anything since the socket connection in the separate thread remains unaffected by the rotation.

Here's what your `MainActivity.java` should look like:

```java
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import android.os.Bundle;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private SocketThread socketThread;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

        String serverIP = "example.com"; // Replace with your server's IP address
        int serverPort = 12345; // Replace with your server's port number

        socketThread = new SocketThread(serverIP, serverPort);
        socketThread.start();
    }

    @Override
    protected void onDestroy() {
        super.onDestroy();
        // Close the socket connection when the activity is destroyed
        if (socketThread != null) {
            socketThread.interrupt();
        }
    }

    // Optionally, handle the configuration changes (e.g., screen rotation)
    // Note: You don't need this method if you don't need to update the UI or resources
    /*
    @Override
    public void onConfigurationChanged(Configuration newConfig) {
        super.onConfigurationChanged(newConfig);

        // Handle configuration changes here
    }
    */
}
```

Now, when the screen rotates, your activity won't be restarted, and the socket connection will remain intact.

To implement network input and output queues accessible from the main activity for the socket thread, you can use a `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` to act as a thread-safe message queue for your socket communication. This way, you will avoid potential synchronization issues between the main activity and the socket thread.

Here's how you can modify the `SocketThread` class and `MainActivity`:

Step 1: Modify the `SocketThread` class

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.net.Socket;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentLinkedQueue;

public class SocketThread extends Thread {
    private final ConcurrentLinkedQueue<String> inputQueue;
    private final ConcurrentLinkedQueue<String> outputQueue;
    private Socket socket;
    private String serverIP;
    private int serverPort;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public SocketThread(String serverIP, int serverPort,
                        ConcurrentLinkedQueue<String> inputQueue,
                        ConcurrentLinkedQueue<String> outputQueue) {
        this.serverIP = serverIP;
        this.serverPort = serverPort;
        this.inputQueue = inputQueue;
        this.outputQueue = outputQueue;
    }

    public void stopRunning() {
        running = false;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            socket = new Socket(serverIP, serverPort);
            PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true);
            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));

            String receivedMessage;

            while (running) {
                // Write messages from outputQueue to the server
                if (!outputQueue.isEmpty()) {
                    String messageToSend = outputQueue.poll();
                    out.println(messageToSend);
                }

                // Read messages from the server and add them to inputQueue
                if (in.ready() && (receivedMessage = in.readLine()) != null) {
                    inputQueue.add(receivedMessage);
                }

                // Sleep for a while to avoid 100% CPU usage
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            }

            out.close();
            in.close();
            socket.close();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

Step 2: Modify the `MainActivity`

```java
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import android.os.Bundle;
import android.widget.Toast;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentLinkedQueue;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private SocketThread socketThread;
    private ConcurrentLinkedQueue<String> inputQueue;
    private ConcurrentLinkedQueue<String> outputQueue;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

        String serverIP = "example.com"; // Replace with your server's IP address
        int serverPort = 12345; // Replace with your server's port number

        inputQueue = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();
        outputQueue = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();

        socketThread = new SocketThread(serverIP, serverPort, inputQueue, outputQueue);
        socketThread.start();

        // Example usage: check for incoming messages every second
        // In a real application, consider using a better method, like a RecyclerView
        // adapter to display messages in a UI element.
        new Thread(() -> {
            while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
                if (!inputQueue.isEmpty()) {
                    String receivedMessage = inputQueue.poll();
                    runOnUiThread(() ->
                            Toast.makeText(this, "Received: " + receivedMessage, Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show());
                }

                try {
                    Thread.sleep(1000);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            }
        }).start();
    }

    @Override
    protected void onDestroy() {
        super.onDestroy();
        // Close the socket connection when the activity is destroyed
        if (socketThread != null) {
            socketThread.stopRunning();
        }
    }

    // Add messages to the output queue to send them to the server
    private void sendMessage(String message) {
        outputQueue.add(message);
    }
}
```

Now, the `inputQueue` and `outputQueue` are accessible from `MainActivity`, and you can add new messages to be sent to the server to the `outputQueue`. Incoming messages from the server will be added to the `inputQueue`. The example above shows a simple usage of the input queue to display a toast with the received message, but in a real application, you can use a more advanced UI to display messages (e.g., RecyclerView).

