Operator overloading in C++ allows you to redefine the behavior of certain operators (such as `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, etc.) for custom data types (classes or structures). This can help make the code more readable and intuitive when working with these custom types. 

Let's consider a simple example of a 2D Vector class with x and y components:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

class Vector2D {
public:
    float x, y;

    Vector2D(float x = 0, float y = 0) : x(x), y(y) {}

    // Overload the '+' operator
    Vector2D operator +(const Vector2D& other) const {
        return Vector2D(x + other.x, y + other.y);
    }

    // Overload the '<<' operator to print vector components
    friend std::ostream& operator <<(std::ostream& stream, const Vector2D& v) {
        stream << "(" << v.x << ", " << v.y << ")";
        return stream;
    }
};

int main() {
    Vector2D v1(3, 4);
    Vector2D v2(1, 2);

    // Use the overloaded '+' operator to add two vectors
    Vector2D v3 = v1 + v2;

    // Use the overloaded '<<' operator to print the vector components
    std::cout << "v1 = " << v1 << std::endl;
    std::cout << "v2 = " << v2 << std::endl;
    std::cout << "v3 = v1 + v2 = " << v3 << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we first overload the `+` operator to allow the addition of two `Vector2D` objects. When we define `operator+`, we can then add two `Vector2D` instances by simply using the `+` operator as we would with built-in data types. In the same manner, we overload the `<<` operator to allow direct output of the `Vector2D` instances to the output stream.

The output of this code would be:

```
v1 = (3, 4)
v2 = (1, 2)
v3 = v1 + v2 = (4, 6)
```

