In ExpressJS, event-based communication between the client and server can be implemented using the built-in `EventEmitter` module in Node.js. Here's a simple example:

First, we'll create an event emitter in the server-side code:

```javascript
const EventEmitter = require('events');

const myEmitter = new EventEmitter();

myEmitter.on('myEvent', (data) => {
    console.log(`Event received with data: ${data}`);
});

// This is just an example, events can be emitted from anywhere in your server code
setTimeout(() => {
    myEmitter.emit('myEvent', 'Hello World!');
}, 5000);
```

This creates an event emitter that listens for `'myEvent'` events and prints the received data to the console. We also emit a `'myEvent'` after 5 seconds with a `'Hello World!'` message.

Next, we'll create a client-side endpoint in our ExpressJS app to listen for these events:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/listenForEvent', (req, res) => {
    const myEmitter = req.app.get('myEmitter');

    myEmitter.on('myEvent', (data) => {
        res.write(`data: ${data}\n\n`);
    });

    req.on('close', () => {
        myEmitter.removeAllListeners('myEvent');
    });
});

app.set('myEmitter', myEmitter);

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log('Server started on port 3000');
});
```

This creates an endpoint at `/listenForEvent` that listens for `'myEvent'` events from the server-side event emitter and sends the received data to the client as a Server-Sent Event using the `res.write()` function. We also remove all event listeners when the client closes the connection using the `req.on('close')` function.

Now we can use any client-side technology that supports Server-Sent Events to listen for events from the server:

```javascript
const source = new EventSource('/listenForEvent');

source.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
    console.log(`Received message: ${event.data}`);
});
```

This receives the `'myEvent'` events from the server and logs the received data to the console.

Note that this is just a simple example, and there are many other ways to implement event-based communication using ExpressJS and Node.js.

Client-side code:

```javascript
const socket = io();   // Connect to server using socket.io

// Send event to server
socket.emit('messageFromClient', { message: 'Hello server!' });

// Listen for event from server
socket.on('messageFromServer', (data) => {
  console.log(data.message);
});
```

Server-side code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const http = require('http').createServer(app);
const io = require('socket.io')(http);

io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  console.log('A user connected.');

  // Listen for event from client
  socket.on('messageFromClient', (data) => {
    console.log(data.message);

    // Send event to client
    io.emit('messageFromServer', { message: 'Hello client!' });
  });

  socket.on('disconnect', () => {
    console.log('A user disconnected.');
  });
});

http.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server is running on http://localhost:3000');
});
```

The above code sets up a simple communication between the client and server using socket.io. When the client sends an event called `messageFromClient` with some data (`{ message: 'Hello server!' }`), the server logs the message and sends a response back to the client with an event called `messageFromServer` and some data (`{ message: 'Hello client!' }`). The client then logs this message. 

This communication is event-based, meaning that the client and server can send and receive events at any time. This allows for real-time updates and a more seamless user experience.

Without using socket.io, one way to implement event-based communication between the client and server is to use the native browser `EventSource` API.

Client-side code:

```javascript
const eventSource = new EventSource('/events');

// Listen for event from server
eventSource.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
  console.log(event.data);
});

// Send event to server
fetch('/events', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ message: 'Hello server!' }),
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
});
```

Server-side code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Listen for GET requests for event stream
app.get('/events', (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');

  // Send "ping" event every 10 seconds to keep connection alive
  const pingInterval = setInterval(() => {
    res.write('event: ping\ndata: \n\n');
  }, 10000);

  // Stop sending events when client closes connection
  req.on('close', () => {
    clearInterval(pingInterval);
    console.log('Client disconnected.');
  });
});

// Listen for POST requests for sending events to client
app.post('/events', (req, res) => {
  const { message } = req.body;

  // Send event to client
  res.write('event: message\ndata: ' + JSON.stringify({ message }) + '\n\n');
  res.end();
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server is running on http://localhost:3000');
});
```

The above code sets up a server that listens for GET requests to `/events` for event streaming. Every 10 seconds, the server sends a "ping" event to keep the connection alive. When the client sends a POST request to `/events` with some data (`{ message: 'Hello server!' }`), the server sends an event with the data to the client using the `event: message` format.

On the client-side, we listen for events from the server using `EventSource`. When the server sends an event, the callback function attached to the `message` event is called with the event data, allowing us to do something with the data.

Note that using `EventSource` is unidirectional (only from server to client) and cannot be used for bidirectional communication. For two-way communication, socket.io or other real-time libraries may be better suited.

