For the actual jumping behaviour of entities on platform logic, you can follow these steps:

1. Make sure the entity isn't already physically touching the platform (check if `obj.y + obj.h < this.y`).
2. If the entity started touching the platform, set `obj.jumping` to `false`, as the entity is no longer jumping.
3. Add a property for the maximum jump height, and if the entity is not touching the platform, set a new target height for that jump based on the maximum height.
4. When the entity is in the air and no longer touching the platform, increment the entity's y position, calculate their x position based on the height, and move the entity in a parabolic arc.
5. Update the velocities based on the new positions and jump force.

Here's an example jump logic update inside the `Entity` class's `update` method:

```javascript
update(platforms) {
  // ... existing code ...

  if (this.jumping) {
    // Calculate the new x and y positions based on the parabolic arc jump
    const jumpForce = 10; // Adjust this value to control the jump force
    const maxJumpHeight = this.y - 50; // Adjust this value to set the max jump height
    const targetY = Math.max(this.y + this.vy, maxJumpHeight);
    this.y = targetY;
    this.x += this.vx;

    // Update velocities based on the new positions
    this.vx -= jumpForce * Math.sin(Math.atan2(targetY - this.y, this.vx));
    this.vy -= jumpForce * Math.cos(Math.atan2(targetY - this.y, this.vx));

    // Check collision with platforms below
    for (let i = 0; i < platforms.length; i++) {
      if (platforms[i].collidesWith(this)) {
        this.jumping = false;
        break;
      }
    }
  }

  // ... remaining code ...
}
