I am getting an error in the Texture::Cleanup method saying "A heap has been corrupted". Do you know what might be causing this error and how to fix it?

The error "A heap has been corrupted" typically occurs when you've written data past the boundaries of an allocated memory block. This could be the result of writing more data than intended or freeing the same memory block multiple times.

Based on the code provided, there might be an issue in the manner the Terrain and Texture objects are destroyed and cleaned up.

In the Terrain destructor, you're calling the `Cleanup()` methods for the terrainMesh and terrainMaterial:

```cpp
Terrain::~Terrain() {
    // Cleanup any resources associated with the Terrain class
    terrainMesh.Cleanup();
    terrainMaterial.Cleanup();
}
```

This is not needed since the `terrainMesh` and `terrainMaterial` members will be destroyed automatically once the Terrain object's destructor is called. But since the Terrain destructor accesses the Mesh and Material's destructors (which call Cleanup), the resources are double-freed, causing a heap corruption.

To fix the issue, remove the `terrainMesh.Cleanup()` and `terrainMaterial.Cleanup()` calls from the Terrain destructor. The destructors of Mesh and Material should handle cleaning up the resources correctly on their own.

