To check if an input matches a specific pattern in JavaScript, you can use the test() method of a regular expression object with the pattern you want to match. 

Here's an example:

```javascript
// Define the regular expression pattern
const myPattern = /^([1-9][0-9]*)$/;

// Get user input from an HTML input field
const userInput = document.getElementById("myInput").value;

// Test if the user input matches the pattern
const isMatch = myPattern.test(userInput);

// Output the result
if (isMatch) {
  console.log("Match found!");
} else {
  console.log("No match found.");
}
```

In this example, we define a regular expression pattern that matches any positive integer. We then use the test() method to check if a user's input matches the pattern. If the input matches, we output "Match found!" to the console, otherwise we output "No match found.".

You can replace the regular expression pattern with any pattern you need to check.

You can use the following regular expression pattern to match strings that start with either a 1 or 2 and have a length of 13 characters:

```javascript
^(1|2)\d{12}$
```

Let's break down how this pattern works:

- `^` Matches the start of the line.
- `(1|2)` Matches either 1 or 2.
- `\d{12}` Matches any digit exactly 12 times.
- `$` Matches the end of the line.

So, this regular expression pattern matches any string that starts with 1 or 2 and has exactly 13 digits.

Here's an example of how to use this regular expression pattern in JavaScript to check if a string matches the pattern:

```javascript
const inputString = "1234567890123"; // replace with your input string

const pattern = /^(1|2)\d{12}$/;
const isMatch = pattern.test(inputString);

if (isMatch) {
    console.log("Input string matches the pattern");
} else {
    console.log("Input string doesn't match the pattern");
}
```

In this example, if the input string matches the pattern, "Input string matches the pattern" is printed to the console. Otherwise, "Input string doesn't match the pattern" is printed.

