To get all records from a result set returned by a MySQL query using Java, you can use the following code snippet:

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    try {
      // Connect to the MySQL database
      String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost/mydatabase";
      String username = "myusername";
      String password = "mypassword";
      Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, username, password);

      // Execute a SELECT query
      Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();
      String query = "SELECT * FROM mytable";
      ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(query);

      // Loop through the results and print each record
      while (rs.next()) {
        int id = rs.getInt("id");
        String name = rs.getString("name");
        Double price = rs.getDouble("price");
        System.out.println(id + " " + name + " " + price);
      }

      // Close the database connection
      conn.close();
    } catch (Exception e) {
      System.out.println(e.getMessage());
    }
  }
}
```

In the above example, after getting a connection to the MySQL database, a statement object is created to execute a SELECT query to retrieve all records from a table named "mytable". The executeQuery() method of the statement object is called to execute the query, and the ResultSet object returned by the method contains all the results.

A loop is then used to iterate through the ResultSet object using the next() method and print each record using the appropriate get*() method for each column of the table.

Finally, the connection to the database is closed using the close() method of the Connection object.

I can help you create an example of an Android application in Kotlin, which uses the entur/android-nfc-lib library to read an NFC Mifare DESFire EV1 tag. Here's how to create the application:

1. Create a new Android project in Android Studio with Kotlin support (or clone an existing Android project written in Kotlin).

2. Add the library as a dependency in your project's build.gradle file:

```gradle
repositories {
    maven { url 'https://jitpack.io' }
}

dependencies {
    implementation 'com.github.entur:android-nfc-lib:v1.0'
}
```

3. Add the following NFC-related permissions to your AndroidManifest.xml file:

```xml
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.NFC" />
<uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.nfc" />
```

4. Create an activity that will handle NFC events. In your MainActivity.kt file, add the following code:

```kotlin
import android.content.Intent
import android.nfc.NfcAdapter
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
import android.os.Bundle
import android.widget.TextView
import com.github.entur:nfc.desfire.cmd.CardStatus
import com.github.entur:nfc.desfire.cmd.DESFireCommand

class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {
    private lateinit var nfcAdapter: NfcAdapter
    private lateinit var textView: TextView

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)

        textView = findViewById(R.id.textView)

        nfcAdapter = NfcAdapter.getDefaultAdapter(this)
        if (nfcAdapter == null) {
            textView.text = "NFC not supported on this device"
            return
        }

        if (!nfcAdapter.isEnabled) {
            textView.text = "Please enable NFC"
            return
        }
    }

    override fun onResume() {
        super.onResume()
        nfcAdapter.enableForegroundDispatch(this, androidNfc.createPendingIntent(), null, null)
    }

    override fun onPause() {
        super.onPause()
        nfcAdapter.disableForegroundDispatch(this)
    }

    override fun onNewIntent(intent: Intent) {
        super.onNewIntent(intent)
        val action = intent.action
        if (NfcAdapter.ACTION_TECH_DISCOVERED == action) {
            readDesfireCard(intent)
        }
    }

    private fun readDesfireCard(intent: Intent) {
        try {
            val desfireCommand = DESFireCommand(intent)
            val cardStatus: CardStatus? = desfireCommand.getCardStatus()

            if (cardStatus != null) {
                textView.text = "Card detected: \nUID: ${cardStatus.uid}"
            } else {
                textView.text = "Card detected: \nUnable to read card status"
            }
        } catch (e: Exception) {
            textView.text = "Error reading card: \n${e.message}"
            e.printStackTrace()
        }
    }
}
```

Replace `R.id.textView` with the ID of your TextView in your layout file.

5. Update your activity_main.xml layout file with a `TextView` with the ID you specified in your MainActivity:

```xml
<TextView
    android:id="@+id/textView"
    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:text="Waiting for NFC Tag..."
    app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
    app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
    app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent" />
```

6. Run the application on an NFC-enabled Android device. When you bring the device closer to a Mifare DESFire EV1 tag, the application should read the tag and display its UID.

Please note that the provided code is just an example and can be further improved depending on your needs. This code should be enough to get you started with reading NFC Mifare DESFire EV1 tags using the entur/android-nfc-lib library.

