OkHttp Client caches the DNS resolution results by default, which can cause it to send requests unevenly among the available backend pods in a Kubernetes cluster. To achieve better round-robin load balancing with the OkHttp Client, you can customize its behavior by implementing a custom `Dns` instance that performs DNS resolution for each request or reduces the DNS cache duration.

Here's an example of how to create a custom `Dns` implementation and configure the OkHttp Client to use it:

```java
import okhttp3.Dns;
import java.net.InetAddress;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

// ...

class RoundRobinDns implements Dns {
    private final AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

    @Override
    public List<InetAddress> lookup(String hostname) throws UnknownHostException {
        List<InetAddress> addresses = Dns.SYSTEM.lookup(hostname);
        Collections.shuffle(addresses, new SecureRandom());
        return addresses;
    }
}

// ...

// Create a custom Dns instance
Dns roundRobinDns = new RoundRobinDns();

// Configure the OkHttpClient to use the custom Dns implementation
OkHttpClient client = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .dns(roundRobinDns)
    .connectionPool(new ConnectionPool(5, 5, TimeUnit.MINUTES))
    .build();
```

In this example, a custom `RoundRobinDns` class is implemented that shuffles the IP addresses returned from the default DNS resolution before returning them. Then, an instance of `RoundRobinDns` is created and assigned to the OkHttpClient builder through the `dns()` method. This should help in achieving better round-robin load balancing in your Kubernetes cluster.

Please note that this example may not provide perfect round-robin load balancing as it still depends on the DNS resolution and caching. It might be better to consider using a LoadBalancer service in your Kubernetes cluster for more advanced load balancing functionality as mentioned in the earlier response.

To achieve the best possible round-robin solution with OkHttp and bypass the default caching behavior, you can implement a custom `Dns` instance that resolves the IP addresses for each request. This can help ensure that your application gets the latest DNS entries for more balanced traffic distribution across backend pods.

```java
import okhttp3.Dns;
import java.net.InetAddress;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

// ...

class RoundRobinDns implements Dns {
    private final AtomicInteger nextIndex = new AtomicInteger();

    @Override
    public List<InetAddress> lookup(String hostname) throws UnknownHostException {
        List<InetAddress> addresses = Dns.SYSTEM.lookup(hostname);
        return getRoundRobinAddress(addresses);
    }

    private List<InetAddress> getRoundRobinAddress(List<InetAddress> addresses) {
        if (addresses.isEmpty()) {
            return addresses;
        }

        List<InetAddress> result = new ArrayList<>(addresses.size());
        int startIndex = nextIndex.getAndUpdate(i -> (i + 1) % addresses.size());
        for (int i = 0; i < addresses.size(); i++) {
            result.add(addresses.get((startIndex + i) % addresses.size()));
        }
        return result;
    }
}

// ...

// Create a custom Dns instance
Dns roundRobinDns = new RoundRobinDns();

// Configure the OkHttpClient to use the custom Dns implementation
OkHttpClient client = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
        .dns(roundRobinDns)
        .build();
```

With this custom `RoundRobinDns` implementation, DNS resolution is performed for each request, and the resolved addresses are rotated using a round-robin algorithm. This should result in more even traffic distribution across your Kubernetes backend pods.

However, please keep in mind that extensive DNS resolution may have a performance impact on your application due to increased DNS lookups. It's recommended to monitor your application's performance and optimize as needed. You may also want to use HTTP/2 with OkHttp if your backend supports it, as it can help improve request concurrency and load balancing further.

An OkHttp Interceptor can't directly influence DNS resolution or perform round-robin distribution. This is because an interceptor operates at the level of HTTP requests and responses, while DNS resolution happens at a lower level (TCP connection).

However, you can combine an interceptor with a custom Dns implementation for better control over load balancing. The interceptor will enforce a new connection for each request, which would work in conjunction with the custom Dns implementation to get a new DNS resolution for every request.

Here's a combination of an interceptor and a custom Dns implementation to achieve round-robin load balancing with OkHttp:

```java
import okhttp3.Interceptor;
import okhttp3.Response;
import okhttp3.Dns;
import java.io.IOException;

// ...

// Create an interceptor that enforces a new connection for each request
Interceptor roundRobinInterceptor = new Interceptor() {
    @Override
    public Response intercept(Chain chain) throws IOException {
        return chain.proceed(chain.request().newBuilder().header("Connection", "close").build());
    }
};

// Configure OkHttpClient to use the custom Dns implementation and interceptor
OkHttpClient client = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
        .dns(roundRobinDns) // roundRobinDns is the custom Dns implementation created earlier
        .addInterceptor(roundRobinInterceptor)
        .build();
```

In the above implementation, we added a new `Connection: close` header to each request in the interceptor by creating a new request builder. This enforces a new connection for each request, which works with the custom Dns solution provided in previous answers to achieve round-robin load balancing.

Still, it's important to note that this approach can negatively impact the performance of your application, as creating a new connection for each request could result in more latency, especially if using TLS. Using HTTP/1 may cause inefficient use of network resources. You might want to enable HTTP/2 if your backend supports this, which will help improve request concurrency and load balancing.

