C++ templates are a powerful feature of the language that allow for generic programming. One of the cool tricks that C++ templates allow for is the Curiously Recurring Template Pattern (CRTP). By using the CRTP, a class can inherit from a template class with itself as the template parameter. This pattern can be used to achieve compile-time polymorphism and provide implementation details in base classes.

Here's an example of how the CRTP can be used:

```cpp
template<typename Derived>
class Base {
public:
  void foo() {
    static_cast<Derived*>(this)->foo_impl(); // Call the derived class implementation
  }
};

class Derived1 : public Base<Derived1> {
public:
  void foo_impl() {
    std::cout << "Derived1::foo_impl()" << std::endl;
  }
};

class Derived2 : public Base<Derived2> {
public:
  void foo_impl() {
    std::cout << "Derived2::foo_impl()" << std::endl;
  }
};

int main() {
  Derived1 d1;
  d1.foo(); // This will call Derived1::foo_impl()

  Derived2 d2;
  d2.foo(); // This will call Derived2::foo_impl()

  return 0;
}
```

In this example, the Base class is a template class that takes a type parameter `Derived`. The `foo()` function is defined in the Base class and calls the `foo_impl()` function of the derived class through a static cast. The `Derived1` and `Derived2` classes inherit from the Base class with themselves as the template parameter. When the `foo()` function is called on an instance of the derived classes, the appropriate implementation in the derived class is called at compile-time.

Another cool trick with C++ templates is the ability to use Variadic templates. Variadic templates allow you to create functions and classes that take a variable number of arguments of different types. This feature can be used to create generic algorithms that can work on any number of arguments of different types.

Here's an example of how Variadic templates can be used:

```cpp
template<typename T>
void print(const T& t) {
  std::cout << t << std::endl;
}

template<typename T, typename... Args>
void print(const T& t, const Args&... args) {
  std::cout << t << ", ";
  print(args...);
}

int main() {
  print("This", "is", "a", "variadic", "template", "example");

  return 0;
}
```

In this example, `print()` is a Variadic template function that takes a variable number of arguments. The base case of the function is defined for when there is only one argument. The recursive case is defined for when there are more than one argument. Each argument is printed out to the console followed by a comma and then the rest of the arguments are passed to the next recursive call until there are no more arguments. This results in the output "This, is, a, variadic, template, example" being printed to the console.

These are just a few examples of the cool tricks that C++ templates can do with CRTP and Variadic templates. There are many more ways that templates can be used to achieve powerful results in C++.

