To create 1000 random scores from an 88 piano score range using javax.sound.midi, you will have to do the following steps: 

1. Create a new `Sequence` instance, which will store your MIDI data.
2. Create a new `Track` in the sequence for your events.
3. Iterate through 1000 random scores, adding `ON` and `OFF` events to the track.

Here's a sample code snippet:

```java
import javax.sound.midi.*;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Random;

public class RandomMidi {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Sequence sequence = new Sequence(Sequence.PPQ, 24);
            Track track = sequence.createTrack();

            Random r = new Random();
            int scoreCount = 1000;
            int tick = 0;

            for (int i = 0; i < scoreCount; i++) {
                int note = 21 + r.nextInt(88); // Random Note (A0 to C8)
                int velocity = 64; // Default velocity
                int duration = 24; // Default duration

                track.add(createMidiEvent(ShortMessage.NOTE_ON, note, velocity, tick)); // On
                track.add(createMidiEvent(ShortMessage.NOTE_OFF, note, 0, tick + duration)); // Off

                tick += duration; // Move to next note
            }

            track.add(createMidiEvent(ShortMessage.NOTE_ON, 0, 0, tick)); // End of track

            MidiSystem.write(sequence, 1, new File("output.mid")); // Save to MIDI file

        } catch (InvalidMidiDataException | IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static MidiEvent createMidiEvent(int command, int note, int velocity, long tick) throws InvalidMidiDataException {
        ShortMessage shortMessage = new ShortMessage();
        shortMessage.setMessage(command, 0, note, velocity);
        return new MidiEvent(shortMessage, tick);
    }
}
```

This Java code will generate a new MIDI file named "output.mid" containing 1000 random piano notes. To add more variation, you can adjust the velocity and duration of the notes as desired.

To create a public WiFi network with a Terms of Service (ToS) page that users must acknowledge before getting access, you will need to set up a "captive portal." A captive portal is a web page that users must interact with before accessing the Internet.

Here's a step-by-step guide on how to set up a captive portal using a router or access point flashed with OpenWRT firmware, which is open-source and highly customizable:

1. Install OpenWRT on your router or access point.

   - Visit the OpenWRT website (https://openwrt.org) and follow the instructions to download and install the appropriate OpenWRT firmware for your device.
   - Ensure that your device is listed as supported on the OpenWRT website before proceeding.

2. Install and set up a web server and PHP on your device.

   - SSH into your router or access point by connecting to it using an SSH client (e.g., PuTTY for Windows or the 'ssh' command on Linux and macOS) and entering its IP address.
   - Update package lists by running: `opkg update`
   - Install the web server (uHTTPd) and PHP by running: `opkg install uhttpd uhttpd-mod-ubus php7 php7-cgi`
   - Change the uHTTPd configuration to enable PHP by editing the '/etc/config/uhttpd' file (you can use `vi` or `nano` text editors). Update the 'list interpreter' line as shown below:
```   
   list interpreter ".php=/usr/bin/php-cgi"
```   
   - Restart the uHTTPd web server to apply changes by running: `/etc/init.d/uhttpd restart`

3. Create the Terms of Service (ToS) page and save it as a PHP file.

   - Create a file named 'tos.php' in the '/www' directory using a text editor (e.g., `vi` or `nano`).
   - Add the HTML and PHP code for your ToS page. Below is a basic example:

```php
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>WiFi Terms of Service</title>
<style>
button {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 10px;
}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<h1>WiFi Terms of Service</h1>
<p>Please read and accept the following terms to use this WiFi network:</p>
<!-- Add the terms text here -->
<p>By clicking "Connect," you agree to these terms.</p>
<button id="acceptBtn" onclick="accept()">Connect</button>
<script>
  function accept() {
    var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
    xhr.open("GET", "/cgi-bin/accept.php?mac=<?php echo $_GET['mac']; ?>", true);
    xhr.send();
    xhr.onload = function() {
      if (xhr.status == 200) {
        window.location.href = "http://www.example.com"; // Redirect the user
      }
    };
  }
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

4. Create a script to authorize users.

   - Create a file named 'accept.php' in the '/www/cgi-bin' directory using a text editor.
   - Add the following PHP code:

```php
<?php
header("Content-Type: text/plain");
if (!isset($_GET['mac'])) {
    echo "Error: no MAC address provided.";
    exit;
}
$mac = $_GET['mac'];
$mac = preg_replace("/[^0-9A-Fa-f:]/", '', $mac); // Remove non-hex and non-colon characters
if (strlen($mac) != 17) {
    echo "Error: invalid MAC address.";
    exit;
}
// Configure your authorization and firewall rules here, e.g.:
shell_exec("uci add dhcp host");
shell_exec("uci set dhcp.@host[-1].mac='$mac'");
shell_exec("uci set dhcp.@host[-1].ip='192.168.1.50'"); // Set an allowed IP address for the user device
shell_exec("uci commit dhcp");
shell_exec("service dnsmasq restart");

// Allow the user's device to access the internet
shell_exec("iptables -I FORWARD -m mac --mac-source $mac -j ACCEPT");

echo "Success";
?>
```
   - Save the file and grant it executable permissions by running: `chmod +x /www/cgi-bin/accept.php`

5. Set up a captive portal.

   - Install the 'nodogsplash' package by running: `opkg install nodogsplash`
   - Edit the '/etc/config/nodogsplash' file and change the following values:

```
   option enabled 1
   option gatewayname "Public WiFi"
   option gatewayaddress "192.168.1.1" # Use your router's IP address
   option maxclients 250
   option preauthidletimeout 10
   option sessiontimeout 10800 # Adjust as needed (e.g., 3h = 10800s)
   option authidletimeout 600 # Adjust as needed (e.g., 10min = 600s)
   option redirectURL "http://www.example.com"
```

   - Add a new 'Splash HTML' section to the bottom of the file:

```
   config splash_html
       option title 'WiFi ToS'
       option heading 'WiFi Terms of Service'
       option imagesdir '/www/images/'
       option info 'Please accept the terms to use this WiFi network.'
       option redirectURL '/tos.php?mac=$mac$'
       option style 'body { background-color: #fff; }'
```

   - Save the file and restart the nodogsplash service by running: `/etc/init.d/nodogsplash restart`

Now your public WiFi network should require users to read and accept the Terms of Service before accessing the internet. After they click "Connect," they will be redirected to the pre-defined redirect URL. Note that you might need to tweak your router's configuration and/or the script based on your specific setup and requirements.

