To set the authenticated user in Spring Security Webflux, you can use the `ReactiveSecurityContextHolder` class. 

Here is an example:

```java
Mono<Void> setCurrentUser(String username, String password) {
    UserDetails userDetails = userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username);
    UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken authenticationToken = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(userDetails, password, userDetails.getAuthorities());
    return ReactiveSecurityContextHolder.getContext()
            .map(SecurityContextImpl::new)
            .doOnNext(securityContext -> securityContext.setAuthentication(authenticationToken))
            .flatMap(securityContextRepository::save)
            .then();
}
```

This method takes in the `username` and `password` of the user and uses them to load the `UserDetails` from the `userDetailsService`. It then creates a new `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken` with the `UserDetails`, password, and authorities.

Next, it uses the `ReactiveSecurityContextHolder` to retrieve the current `SecurityContext` and creates a new `SecurityContextImpl` with the authentication token. It then sets the authentication on the new `SecurityContext` and saves it using the provided `securityContextRepository`.

This method returns a `Mono<Void>` to indicate when the operation has completed.

Here is an example of how to set the authentication user in Spring Security Webflux:

```java
public Mono<Void> setAuthentication(String username, String password) {
    UserDetails userDetails = userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username);
    UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken authenticationToken = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(userDetails, password, userDetails.getAuthorities());
    return Mono.just(authenticationToken)
            .flatMap(authenticationManager::authenticate)
            .doOnNext(SecurityContextHolder::setContext)
            .subscribeOn(Schedulers.parallel())
            .then();
}
```

In this example, the `userDetailsService` is used to load the `UserDetails` by the provided `username`. Then, an instance of `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken` is created with the `UserDetails`, `password`, and `authorities`.

Next, the `authenticationToken` is authenticated using the `authenticationManager`, which is typically an instance of `ReactiveAuthenticationManager`. The result is a `Mono<Authentication>` which is the authenticated token.

Once the token is authenticated, the `SecurityContextHolder` is set with the authenticated token by invoking the `setContext` method. Lastly, the operation is performed asynchronously using the `subscribeOn` operator on a `Schedulers.parallel()` scheduler, and returns a `Mono<Void>` to indicate when the operation is completed.

To authenticate a user using `ReactiveSecurityContextHolder` in Spring Security Webflux, you can use the `ReactiveAuthenticationManager` interface.

Here is an example:

```java
public Mono<Void> authenticateUser(String username, String password) {
    UserDetails userDetails = userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username);
    UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken token = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(userDetails, password, userDetails.getAuthorities());
    return Mono.just(token)
            .flatMap(authenticationManager::authenticate)
            .doOnNext(SecurityContextHolder::setContext)
            .then();
}
```

In this example, the `userDetailsService` is used to load the `UserDetails` for the user by the provided `username`. Then, an instance of `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken` is created with the `UserDetails`, `password`, and `authorities`.

Next, a `Mono` is created with the authentication token and passed to the `authenticate` method of the `authenticationManager`. The `authenticationManager` is typically an instance of `ReactiveAuthenticationManager`.

If the authentication is successful, the resulting authenticated `Authentication` object is set in the `SecurityContextHolder` by invoking the `setContext` method. The method returns a `Mono<Void>` to indicate when the operation is complete.

