`__builtin_va_start` is a built-in function in C and C++ programming languages that initializes a `va_list` object that can hold a variable number of arguments passed to a function. 

Syntax:
```c
void __builtin_va_start(va_list arg_list, parameter);
```

**Parameters:**
- `arg_list`: A `va_list` type object that needs to be initialized.
- `parameter`: The name of the last fixed parameter of the function, before the variable argument list.

**Return Value:**
The function does not return any value. It initializes the `va_list`.

**Example:**

```c
#include <stdarg.h>
#include <stdio.h>

void print_ints(int count, ...) {
    va_list arg_list;
    int i;

    /* Initialize the va_list */
    __builtin_va_start(arg_list, count);

    for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        int val = __builtin_va_arg(arg_list, int); 
        printf("%d\n", val);
    }

    /* Clean up the va_list */
    __builtin_va_end(arg_list);
}

int main() {
    print_ints(3, 10, 20, 30);
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, `__builtin_va_start` initializes the `va_list` object `arg_list` and `__builtin_va_arg` is used to retrieve the variable arguments one by one. Once all variable arguments are processed, we must call the `__builtin_va_end` function to free up any resources associated with the `va_list`.

