Currently, there is no direct method to close all open modals programmatically at once. However, you can use the `views.update` method to close individual modals by updating them with empty views.

To do this, you need to store the `trigger_id` or `view_id` of each open modal when they are opened. Then, for each modal you want to close, call the `views.update` method with an empty view.

Here's a sample implementation using Slack Bolt and TypeScript:

```typescript
import { App, LogLevel } from '@slack/bolt';

const app = new App({
  token: process.env.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN,
  signingSecret: process.env.SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET,
  logLevel: LogLevel.DEBUG,
});

// Store trigger_ids or view_ids of open modals
const openModals: string[] = [];

// Sample listener that opens a modal
app.shortcut('sample_modal', async ({ ack, body, client }) => {
  await ack();

  try {
    const result = await client.views.open({
      trigger_id: body.trigger_id,
      view: {
        type: 'modal',
        callback_id: 'sample_modal_view',
        title: {
          type: 'plain_text',
          text: 'Sample Modal',
        },
        blocks: [
          // Your modal blocks go here
        ],
      },
    });

    // Save the view_id of the opened modal
    const viewId: string = (result as any).view.id;
    openModals.push(viewId);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
});

// Function to close all open modals
async function closeAllOpenModals(client: any) {
  for (const viewId of openModals) {
    try {
      await client.views.update({
        view_id: viewId,
        view: {
          type: 'modal',
          blocks: [],
        },
      });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  }

  // Clear the list of open modals
  openModals.length = 0;
}

// Sample listener to close all open modals upon a specific command
app.command('/closemodals', async ({ ack, client }) => {
  await ack();
  await closeAllOpenModals(client);
});

(async () => {
  const server = await app.start(process.env.PORT || 3000);
  console.log(`Server is running on ${server.address().port}`);
})();
```

This example adds a modal to the `openModals` array using the `view_id` property whenever a modal is opened. The `closeAllOpenModals` function is designed to loop through the `openModals` array and use the `views.update` method to close each modal by providing an empty view. When running the `/closemodals` command, all open modals are closed simultaneously.

No, you cannot open a modal without a `trigger_id`. The `trigger_id` is required to associate the modal with a specific user action that triggers it, such as a button click, slash command, or interaction with a block element.

When you attempt to open a modal, the Slack API requires a `trigger_id` to be passed along with other view properties. The `trigger_id` can be obtained from the payload delivered by the user action.

For example, when responding to a slash command, you can find the `trigger_id` in the body of the incoming request like this:

```javascript
app.command('/open_modal', async ({ ack, body, client }) => {
  await ack();
  const triggerId = body.trigger_id;

  // Use triggerId to open the modal
});
```

For a button click, you can get the `trigger_id` from the action payload:

```javascript
app.action('open_modal_button', async ({ ack, body, client }) => {
  await ack();
  const triggerId = body.trigger_id;

  // Use triggerId to open the modal
});
```

In both cases, you need the `trigger_id` to open a modal. For more information about modals and the `views.open` method, refer to the [official documentation](https://api.slack.com/methods/views.open).

