You can use `tokio::process::Command` instead of `std::process::Command` and utilize its `spawn()` method to stream the command's output. Here's the modified version of your `handle_push_path` function:

```rust
use tokio::io::AsyncReadExt;
use tokio::process::Command;

async fn handle_push_path(
    &self,
    path: &Path,
    req_path: &str,
    is_dir: bool,
    rclone_path: Option<PathBuf>,
    head_only: bool,
    user: Option<String>,
    res: &mut Response,
) -> Result<()> {
    let parent_path = Path::new(req_path).parent().unwrap();
    println!("local:{}", req_path);
    println!("remote:{}", parent_path.to_str().unwrap());
    let out = if is_dir { req_path } else { parent_path.to_str().unwrap() };

    let mut command = Command::new(rclone_path.unwrap().to_str().unwrap())
        .arg("copy")
        .arg(format!("local:{}", req_path))
        .arg(format!("remote:{}", out))
        .arg("-P")
        .arg("-M")
        .arg("--create-empty-src-dirs")
        .stdout(Stdio::piped())
        .stderr(Stdio::piped())
        .spawn()?;

    let mut stdout = String::new();
    let mut stderr = String::new();

    if let Some(mut stdout_stream) = command.stdout.take() {
        stdout_stream.read_to_string(&mut stdout).await?;
    }

    if let Some(mut stderr_stream) = command.stderr.take() {
        stderr_stream.read_to_string(&mut stderr).await?;
    }

    let status = command.wait().await?;

    let json_output: Value = json!({
        "status": status.success(),
        "stdout": stdout.trim(),
        "stderr": stderr.trim(),
    });

    res.headers_mut()
        .typed_insert(ContentType::from(mime_guess::mime::APPLICATION_JSON));
    res.headers_mut()
        .typed_insert(ContentLength(json_output.to_string().as_bytes().len() as u64));
    if head_only {
        return Ok(());
    }
    *res.body_mut() = json_output.to_string().into();
    Ok(())
}
```

In this version, the `Command` is spawned with the `spawn()` method, and then, the JSON output is streamed to the client while the command is running.

There are a few ways to log a `&[u8]` to a string in Rust, depending on what you're trying to achieve.

1. Convert the `&[u8]` to a `String`

If you want to log the byte slice as a string, you can convert it to a `String` using the `from_utf8_lossy()` method. This converts the byte slice to a UTF-8 encoded string, replacing any invalid UTF-8 sequences with the `U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER`:

```rust
use std::str;

fn main() {
    let bytes: &[u8] = b"Hello, world!";
    let string = String::from_utf8_lossy(bytes);
    println!("{}", string);
}
```

This will print `Hello, world!`.

2. Print the byte slice as hexadecimal

If you want to log the byte slice as a series of hexadecimal values, you can use the `hex::encode()` function from the `hex` crate:

```rust
use hex;

fn main() {
    let bytes: &[u8] = b"Hello, world!";
    let hex_string = hex::encode(bytes);
    println!("{}", hex_string);
}
```

This will print `48656c6c6f2c20776f726c6421`.

3. Print the byte slice as base64-encoded

If you want to log the byte slice as a base64-encoded string, you can use the `base64::encode()` function from the `base64` crate:

```rust
use base64;

fn main() {
    let bytes: &[u8] = b"Hello, world!";
    let base64_string = base64::encode(bytes);
    println!("{}", base64_string);
}
```

This will print `SGVsbG8sIHdvcmxkIQ==`.

