An enum in C++ is a keyword that is used to declare an enumerated data type. It is used to define a set of named integer constants. Enum helps in making the code more readable, as it replaces the hard-coded numeric values with descriptive names. Here is how to use enum in C++:

Syntax:

```cpp
enum enum_name {
    constant1,
    constant2,
    constant3,
    ...
};
```

Example:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Declare an enum named Color with 3 named constants
enum Color {RED, BLUE, GREEN};

int main() {
    // Declare a variable of Color type
    Color c = RED;

    // Print the value of the enum variable
    if (c == RED) {
        cout << "The color is RED." << endl;
    }
    else if (c == BLUE) {
        cout << "The color is BLUE." << endl;
    }
    else if (c == GREEN) {
        cout << "The color is GREEN." << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In the above example, we have declared an enum named Color with three named constants: RED, BLUE, and GREEN. We then declare a variable named c of type Color and assign the value RED to it. We then check the value of c using if-else statements to print the corresponding color name.

Here is an example of a SinglyLinkedList class with the swapTwoNodes method added to it. The main method is also provided to test the swapTwoNodes method with various inputs.

```java
class SinglyLinkedList {
    Node head;

    static class Node {
        int data;
        Node next;

        Node(int d) {
            data = d;
            next = null;
        }
    }

    void swapTwoNodes(int x, int y) {
        if (x == y) return;

        Node prevX = null, currX = head;
        while (currX != null && currX.data != x) {
            prevX = currX;
            currX = currX.next;
        }

        Node prevY = null, currY = head;
        while (currY != null && currY.data != y) {
            prevY = currY;
            currY = currY.next;
        }

        if (currX == null || currY == null)
            return;

        if (prevX != null)
            prevX.next = currY;
        else
            head = currY;

        if (prevY != null)
            prevY.next = currX;
        else
            head = currX;

        Node temp = currX.next;
        currX.next = currY.next;
        currY.next = temp;
    }

    void push(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);
        newNode.next = head;
        head = newNode;
    }

    void printList() {
        Node temp = head;
        while (temp != null) {
            System.out.print(temp.data + " ");
            temp = temp.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SinglyLinkedList list = new SinglyLinkedList();

        list.push(7);
        list.push(6);
        list.push(5);
        list.push(4);
        list.push(3);
        list.push(2);
        list.push(1);

        System.out.println("Linked list before swapping");
        list.printList();

        list.swapTwoNodes(4, 3);
        System.out.println("Linked list after swapping");
        list.printList();

        list.swapTwoNodes(1, 7);
        System.out.println("Linked list after swapping");
        list.printList();

        list.swapTwoNodes(2, 2);
        System.out.println("Linked list after swapping");
        list.printList();
    }
}
```

This code defines a SinglyLinkedList class with a Node inner class to represent the structure of a linked list. The swapTwoNodes method first checks whether the given reference elements are the same or not. It then proceeds with finding the nodes containing the given reference elements in the list. Finally, if both the nodes are found, it swaps their positions within the list without swapping their contents.

The main method demonstrates the functionality of the swapTwoNodes method by creating a singly linked list and swapping nodes based on different elements.

