This code creates an access point using your specified SSID and password. You can connect to this access point using any WiFi-enabled device. Once connected, open a browser and go to the IP address displayed on the Serial Monitor (usually 192.168.4.1). The web page will show a button that you can use to toggle the LED connected to GPIO 5 on and off. The LED state will be updated on the webpage without refreshing, thanks to AJAX.

The Arduino Nano RP2040 Connect board does not have built-in Wi-Fi capabilities like the ESP8266. To create an access point that can be used to control an LED using AJAX with this board, you will need to use an external Wi-Fi module, such as the ESP8266. Here is an example of how you can create an access point to control an LED using AJAX with the Nano RP2040 Connect and an ESP8266 module:

1. Connect the ESP8266 module to the Arduino Nano RP2040 Connect board as follows:

   - ESP8266 VCC to 3.3V
   - ESP8266 GND to GND
   - ESP8266 TX to D0 (RX)
   - ESP8266 RX to D1 (TX)
   - LED to GPIO 2 (with resistor) and GND

2. Upload the following code to the Arduino Nano RP2040 Connect:

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Arduino_ESP8266.h>

#define SDA_PIN 4
#define SCL_PIN 5

const int ledPin = 2;
bool ledState = LOW;

Arduino_ESP8266 esp8266(SDA_PIN, SCL_PIN);

String getStatePage() {
  String html = "<!DOCTYPE html><html><head>";
  html += "<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">";
  html += "<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:;base64,iVBORw0KGgo=\"><style>html { font-family: Helvetica; display: inline-block; margin: 0px auto; text-align: center;}";
  html += " .button { background-color: #4CAF50; border: none; color:white;padding:8px 32px;text-align:center;text-decoration:none;display:inline-block;font-size:30px;margin:4px;cursor:pointer;width:90%;outline:none;} </style></head>";
  html += "<body><h1>Arduino Nano RP2040 LED Control</h1>";
  html += "<p>LED State: <span id=\"state\">" + String(ledState ? "ON" : "OFF") + "</span></p><p>";
  html += "<button class=\"button\" onclick=\"toggleLED()\">Toggle LED</button>";
  html += "<script>function toggleLED() { var xhttp = new XMLHttpRequest(); xhttp.onreadystatechange = function() {";
  html += "if (this.readyState == 4 && this.status == 200) { document.getElementById(\"state\").innerHTML = this.responseText }";
  html += "}; xhttp.open(\"GET\", \"/toggle\", true); xhttp.send(); } </script></p>";
  html += "</body></html>";

  return html;
}

void receiveEvent(int howMany) {
  int receivedData = Wire.read();

  if (receivedData == 0) {
    ledState = !ledState;
    digitalWrite(ledPin, ledState);
  }
}

void setup() {
  Wire.begin(SDA_PIN, SCL_PIN);
  Wire.onReceive(receiveEvent);

  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);

  esp8266.configureEsp8266AsAccessPoint("MyArduinoAP", "mypassword");
  esp8266.createServer(80, "/", getStatePage);
  esp8266.createServer(80, "/toggle", []() {
    Wire.beginTransmission(4);
    Wire.write(0);
    Wire.endTransmission();

    return ledState ? "ON" : "OFF";
  });
}

void loop() {
  esp8266.handleClient();
}

```

This code sets up a Wi-Fi access point using the ESP8266 module, and serves an HTML page with AJAX to control an LED connected to the RP2040 board's GPIO 2.

