A transition can only occur between two states. The `transition: background-color 15s, color 15s;` rule you provided tells the browser to animate the background color and text color when there's a change from one state to another. However, subsequent changes won't trigger the transition again, as there is no more change.

To achieve an infinite looping transition without using `@keyframes`, one option would be to use the `transitionend` event to trigger the transition again. Here's an example:

HTML:
```html
<div class="box"></div>
```

CSS:
```css
.box {
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
  background-color: red;
  color: white;
  transition: background-color 1s, color 1s;
}
```

JavaScript:
```javascript
const box = document.querySelector('.box');

box.addEventListener('transitionend', () => {
  box.style.backgroundColor = 'blue';
  box.style.color = 'black';
})
```

This code would make the `.box` element transition from red to blue and from white to black. When the transition ends, the event listener would trigger the same transition again and so on, creating an infinite loop.

I apologize for my previous response. It is possible to create a looping transition without using `@keyframes` or JavaScript by using a hack involving CSS pseudo-elements and a CSS counter.

Here's an example:

HTML:
```html
<div class="box"></div>
```

CSS:
```css
.box {
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
  background-color: red;
  color: white;
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
}

.box:before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background-color: blue;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 1s;
  z-index: -1;
}

.box:after {
  content: counter(loop-counter);
  color: transparent;
  font-size: 0;
  counter-reset: loop-counter;
}

.box:hover:before {
  opacity: 1;

  /* Increment the loop counter */
  counter-increment: loop-counter;

  /* Set the pseudo-element background color 
     to the same as the box's background color */
  background-color: red;

  /* Reset the opacity to 0 after the transition ends */
  transition: opacity 1s, background-color 0s 1s;
}
```

This CSS code creates a pseudo-element before the `.box` element, with a background color that transitions from blue to the same color as the `.box` element. When the pseudo-element's opacity reaches 1, it triggers a counter increment, which starts the transition again. The loop counter value is displayed on the `.box:after` element using the `counter()` function.

This hack is complex and not very intuitive, so I would recommend using `@keyframes` or JavaScript for animation and transitions.

