Based on your provided code, you are running an infinite loop (while loop) on a new thread to listen for updates from the server. However, the main thread executing the game UI is blocked from processing UI events because the loop runs indefinitely.

To resolve this issue, you can add a socket timeout to unblock the thread temporarily and allow UI updates. Follow these steps:

1. Set a socket timeout under your existing socket initialization:

   ```java
   socket = new Socket(HOST, PORT);
   socket.setSoTimeout(100);
   ```

   The timeout is set in milliseconds. Setting it to 100 milliseconds is sufficient for this example.

2. Modify the while loop inside the new thread to catch the `SocketTimeoutException`. This exception will be thrown when the loop times out, allowing the UI to process events and then retry:

   ```java
   new Thread(() -> {
       try {
           while (!gameOver) {
               try {
                   // Your existing loop code...
               } catch (SocketTimeoutException e) {
                   // Ignore the timeout exception - this is expected and will allow the UI to update.
                   // No need to call e.printStackTrace().
               }
           }
       } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
           e.printStackTrace();
       }
   }).start();
   ```

These changes should allow the button event handlers to execute and display the board updates in the UI.

