Dynamic Link Libraries (DLLs) are shared libraries that can be loaded and used by different programs at runtime. They contain code and data that can be shared among multiple applications. There are two types of DLLs: static and dynamic.

Static libraries are linked into the executable file during the build process. They increase the size of the executable file but are faster because they can be accessed directly from memory without the need for loading and unloading.

Dynamic libraries, on the other hand, are loaded and unloaded at runtime. They are smaller in size but the loading and unloading process can take some time. They can be updated without having to recompile the entire project.

To create a DLL in WinAPI C++, follow these steps:

1. Create a new project in Visual Studio.
2. Select Win32 Project and choose a DLL option.
3. Select DLL and Empty Project options in the next dialog window.
4. Define the DLL entry point as DllMain function.
5. Define the function signatures that the DLL will expose to other applications.

To use the DLL in your application, you need to declare the functions in your code and link against the DLL at runtime. Here is an example code snippet:

1. Declare the functions in your code:

    ```C++
    #include "mydll.h"
    #include <Windows.h>

    int main()
    {
        HMODULE hDll = LoadLibrary("mydll.dll");
        if (hDll == NULL) {
            // error handling
            return 1;
        }

        typedef void (*MyFuncPtr)();
        MyFuncPtr myFunc = (MyFuncPtr)GetProcAddress(hDll, "myFunc");
        if (myFunc == NULL) {
            // error handling
            return 1;
        }

        // call the function
        myFunc();

        // free the DLL
        FreeLibrary(hDll);

        return 0;
    }
    ```

2. Link against the DLL at runtime by specifying its name in the code.

    ```C++
    #pragma comment(lib, "mydll.lib")
    ```

