Setting up a simple ball game with GLUT and OpenGL in C++ will require a basic knowledge of OpenGL concepts and familiarity with C++. Below is a sample code that demonstrates how to create a simple ball game in C++ using the GLUT and OpenGL libraries. This code will create a window with a ball, which bounces against the walls of the window.

To set up this code on your system, make sure you have the GLUT and OpenGL libraries installed. Please have a look at the guidelines for your specific platform to get started:

1. [Windows guide](http://www.cs.ukzn.ac.za/~hughm/ap/glut/glut3.html)
2. [Linux guide](https://www.glfw.org/docs/latest/compile_guide.html)
3. [Mac guide](https://vicrucann.github.io/tutorials/osx-glut-window/)

After you set up your environment, create a new C++ file and paste the following code:

```cpp
#include <GL/glut.h>

float ballPositionX = 0.0f, ballPositionY = 0.0f;
float ballSpeedX = 0.05f, ballSpeedY = 0.09f;

void display() {
    glClear(GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT);
    glLoadIdentity();

    glTranslatef(ballPositionX, ballPositionY, 0.0f);

    glColor3f(1.0, 1.0, 1.0);
    glBegin(GL_TRIANGLE_FAN);
        glVertex2f(0.0f, 0.0f);
        for (int angle = 0; angle <= 360; angle += 10) {
            float radians = angle * 3.14159 / 180.0;
            float x = cos(radians) * 0.1;
            float y = sin(radians) * 0.1;
            glVertex2f(x, y);
        }
    glEnd();

    glutSwapBuffers();
}

void timer(int = 0) {
    ballPositionX += ballSpeedX;
    ballPositionY += ballSpeedY;

    if (ballPositionX <= -1.0 || ballPositionX >= 1.0) {
        ballSpeedX = -ballSpeedX;
    }
    if (ballPositionY <= -1.0 || ballPositionY >= 1.0) {
        ballSpeedY = -ballSpeedY;
    }

    glutTimerFunc(20, timer, 0);
    glutPostRedisplay();
}

void init() {
    glClearColor(0.2, 0.2, 0.2, 1.0);
}

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    glutInit(&argc, argv);
    glutInitDisplayMode(GLUT_DOUBLE | GLUT_RGB);
    glutInitWindowSize(800, 600);
    glutInitWindowPosition(100, 100);
    glutCreateWindow("Simple Ball Game");
    init();
    glutDisplayFunc(display);
    timer();
    glutMainLoop();
    return 0;
}
```

This code creates a simple ball game with a ball that bounces off the edges of the window. It uses the `glutTimerFunc()` function to update the position of the ball every 20 milliseconds, and it calculates the new position for the ball based on its current position and speed in the X and Y directions.

Compile and run the code, and you should see the ball bouncing off the walls of the window. This is a basic example, and you can further extend it to make more complex and interactive games.

