@Table(name = "address")
public class Address {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String street;

    private String city;

    @OneToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "student_id")
    private Student student;

    // getters and setters
}
```

In the above example, both the Student and Address entities are both the source and target entities. The `@OneToOne` annotation on the Student entity indicates that each Student has only one Address, and the `mappedBy` attribute specifies the Foreign Key column to link the two entities. The `@OneToOne` annotation on the Address entity indicates that each Address belongs to a single Student, and the `@JoinColumn` annotation specifies the Foreign Key column to link the two entities.

4. @ManyToMany:

Consider the following example of a book and its authors. A book can have multiple authors, and an author can write multiple books.

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "book")
public class Book {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String title;

    @ManyToMany
    @JoinTable(
        name = "book_author",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "book_id"),
