To add Vulkan validation layers, you need to enable the `VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation` layer. You have already set up the validation layers vector in your code, but the initialization is inside an `#ifdef` block. Make sure you have defined `NDEBUG` when running in release mode and not defined it when running in debug mode.

Here's a complete list of steps to enable the Vulkan Validation Layers in your code:

1. Make sure you have the LunarG Vulkan SDK installed on your system. It provides the Validation Layers binaries. You can download it from [LunarG's website](https://vulkan.lunarg.com/sdk/home).

2. Make sure that the `NDEBUG` flag is not defined when you're building your application in Debug mode. This will enable the `enableValidationLayers` flag in your code.

3. In the `CreateInstance()` function, before the `vkCreateInstance` call, add the following code to check for validation layer support:

```cpp
if (enableValidationLayers) {
    // Check if validation layers are supported
    uint32_t layerCount;
    vkEnumerateInstanceLayerProperties(&layerCount, nullptr);
    std::vector<VkLayerProperties> availableLayers(layerCount);
    vkEnumerateInstanceLayerProperties(&layerCount, availableLayers.data());

    for (const char* layerName : validationLayers) {
        bool layerFound = false;

        for (const auto& layerProperties : availableLayers) {
            if (strcmp(layerName, layerProperties.layerName) == 0) {
                layerFound = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (!layerFound) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Validation layer requested, but it’s not available.");
        }
    }
}
```

4. If the validation layers are supported, enable them during the instance creation by updating the `createInfo` object:

```cpp
if (enableValidationLayers) {
    createInfo.enabledLayerCount = static_cast<uint32_t>(validationLayers.size());
    createInfo.ppEnabledLayerNames = validationLayers.data();
} else {
    createInfo.enabledLayerCount = 0;
}
```

5. Build and run your application in Debug mode. The Vulkan Validation Layers should now provide debugging information about your application during runtime. Make sure to check your console or debugger output for any error or warning messages.

These changes are enough to enable validation layers. However, if you want to see more information during the debugging process, you can implement a custom Vulkan debug callback function to capture the validation layer messages. You can find more information on how to do this in the official [Vulkan Tutorial](https://vulkan-tutorial.com/Drawing_a_triangle/Setup/Validation_layers#page_Debug_callback).

In Dart, you can't inherit from multiple classes, but you can use a combination of interfaces and mixins to achieve the desired functionality. Here's an example:

```dart
abstract class MyBase {
  // Field
  int _value;

  // Getter and setter
  int get value => _value;
  set value(int newValue) => _value = newValue;

  // Regular method
  void displayValue() {
    print('Value: $_value');
  }

  // Pure virtual method
  void pureVirtualMethod();
}

// You can create an interface that inherits from MyBase so that the inheriting class will need to implement pureVirtualMethod()
abstract class MyBaseInterface implements MyBase {
  void pureVirtualMethod();
}

// Mixin that implements regular methods and getters/setters from MyBase
mixin MyBaseMixin on MyBaseInterface {
  // Field
  int _value;

  // Getter and setter
  int get value => _value;
  set value(int newValue) => _value = newValue;

  // Regular method
  void displayValue() {
    print('Value: $_value');
  }
}

// Your main class that inherits from other class
class MainClass extends AnotherClass with MyBaseMixin implements MyBaseInterface {
  // Implement pure virtual method
  @override
  void pureVirtualMethod() {
    print('Pure virtual method implementation in MainClass');
  }
}

// Example of another class
class AnotherClass {
  void anotherClassMethod() {
    print('Method in AnotherClass');
  }
}

void main() {
  var mainClass = MainClass();
  mainClass.value = 10;
  mainClass.displayValue(); // Output: Value: 10
  mainClass.pureVirtualMethod(); // Output: Pure virtual method implementation in MainClass
  mainClass.anotherClassMethod(); // Output: Method in AnotherClass
}
```

In this example, `MyBase` declares fields, getters/setters, regular methods, and pure virtual methods. `MyBaseInterface` is an interface that inherits from `MyBase`. `MyBaseMixin` implements regular methods and fields from `MyBase`. Finally, `MainClass` extends `AnotherClass`, uses `MyBaseMixin` to include regular methods and field implementations, and implements `MyBaseInterface` to provide the correct contract for the pure virtual methods.

Indeed, having fields and default methods in interfaces would make this process easier and more straightforward in Dart. Such a feature would enable users to define a single interface with fields, and default and pure virtual methods without needing to create separate mixins.

For example, in languages like Java, you can have default methods in interfaces:

```java
public interface MyInterface {

    int getValue();
    void setValue(int newValue);

    default void displayValue() {
        System.out.println("Value: " + getValue());
    }

    void pureVirtualMethod();
}
```

However, Dart doesn't support this directly. But using mixins, as shown in the previous example, is a solution that allows for a similar functionality. It would be a great addition to the Dart language if fields and default methods were allowed in interfaces to simplify this process.

Yes, that's correct. C++ is one of the few languages that allow default methods and storing data in interfaces. In C++, you can achieve this through abstract classes, which can act as interfaces with default methods and data members. Here is an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// `MyInterface` acts as an interface with default methods and data members
class MyInterface {
public:
    virtual ~MyInterface() = default;

    int getValue() const { return value; }
    void setValue(int newValue) { value = newValue; }

    void displayValue() const {
        std::cout << "Value: " << value << std::endl;
    }

    virtual void pureVirtualMethod() = 0;

protected:
    int value;
};

// Inherited from `MyInterface`
class MyClass : public MyInterface {
public:
    void pureVirtualMethod() override {
        std::cout << "Pure virtual method implementation in MyClass" << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    MyClass myClass;
    myClass.setValue(10);
    myClass.displayValue(); // Output: Value: 10
    myClass.pureVirtualMethod(); // Output: Pure virtual method implementation in MyClass

    return 0;
}
```

In this C++ example, `MyInterface` acts as an interface with default methods (`getValue`, `setValue`, and `displayValue`), data members (`value`), and pure virtual methods (`pureVirtualMethod`). Then, `MyClass` inherits from `MyInterface` and implements the pure virtual method.

