Sure! The first step to using a C++ object-oriented backend library in a React Electron frontend is to write a C++ wrapper for your library. 

This wrapper will expose the functionality of your library to Node.js, which will allow you to call it from your Electron app.

Here's an example of what a C++ wrapper might look like:

```cpp
#include "BackendLibrary.hpp"

// Wrapper function to call the BackendLibrary's initialize method
extern "C" void initializeBackend()
{
    BackendLibrary::getInstance().initialize();
}

// Wrapper function to call the BackendLibrary's process method
extern "C" void processBackend()
{
    BackendLibrary::getInstance().process();
}

// Wrapper function to call the BackendLibrary's getResult method
extern "C" int getResultFromBackend()
{
    return BackendLibrary::getInstance().getResult();
}
```

In the above example, we're assuming that our backend library is called `BackendLibrary` and has `initialize()`, `process()`, and `getResult()` methods. We've defined three wrapper functions that correspond to each of these methods.

Each wrapper function is declared with the `extern "C"` keyword, which tells the compiler not to use C++ name mangling. This means that these functions will have the same name when called from Node.js as they do in the C++ code.

Once you've written your C++ wrapper, you'll need to compile it into a dynamic library that you can load into your Node.js app. You can do this using a C++ build system like CMake or Make.

