        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content" />
```

In gtkmm, you can create a custom widget by subclassing the Gtk::Widget class and implementing your own drawing code. Here are the basic steps to create a custom widget:

1. Create a new class that inherits from Gtk::Widget.
2. Override the default constructor as well as the minimum and natural size virtual functions.
3. In the overriding implementation of the minimum and natural size virtual functions, return the minimum and natural size of your custom widget, respectively.
4. Override the on_size_allocate virtual function. This is where you can get the height and width of your widget and use it to set the size of the widget.
5. Override the on_draw virtual function. This function is called whenever the widget needs to be redrawn. Here, you can add your own drawing code to create the custom look of your widget.
6. Add any signals that your widget needs to emit. You can define custom signals by using the `sigc::signal` template class.

Here's an example of a custom widget class in gtkmm:

```c++
#include <gtkmm.h>

class MyCustomWidget : public Gtk::Widget {
public:
    MyCustomWidget() {}
    virtual ~MyCustomWidget() {}

    virtual Gtk::SizeRequestMode get_request_mode_vfunc() const override {
        return Gtk::SizeRequestMode::CONSTANT_SIZE;
    }

    virtual void get_preferred_width_vfunc(int& minimum_width, int& natural_width) const override {
        minimum_width = 50;
        natural_width = 100;
    }

    virtual void get_preferred_height_for_width_vfunc(int width, int& minimum_height, int& natural_height) const override {
        minimum_height = 50;
        natural_height = 100;
    }

    virtual void get_preferred_height_vfunc(int& minimum_height, int& natural_height) const override {
        minimum_height = 50;
        natural_height = 100;
    }

    virtual void get_preferred_width_for_height_vfunc(int height, int& minimum_width, int& natural_width) const override {
        minimum_width = 50;
        natural_width = 100;
    }

protected:
    virtual void on_size_allocate(Gtk::Allocation& allocation) override {
        set_allocation(allocation);
    }

    virtual void on_draw(const Cairo::RefPtr<Cairo::Context>& context) override {
        context->set_source_rgb(1.0, 0.0, 0.0);
        context->rectangle(get_allocation().get_x(), get_allocation().get_y(), get_allocation().get_width(), get_allocation().get_height());
        context->fill();
    }
