Yes, the Rhino JavaScript engine can parse JSON strings. Rhino provides a JSON library that can be used to parse and stringify JSON data.

Here's an example code that demonstrates how to parse a JSON string using Rhino:

```java
import org.mozilla.javascript.Context;
import org.mozilla.javascript.NativeArray;
import org.mozilla.javascript.NativeObject;
import org.mozilla.javascript.Scriptable;
import org.mozilla.javascript.json.JsonParser;

public class ParseJSON {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String jsonString = "{\"name\":\"John\",\"age\":25,\"city\":\"New York\"}";
        Context cx = Context.enter();
        Scriptable scope = cx.initStandardObjects();

        try {
            // Create a new JSON parser object
            JsonParser parser = new JsonParser(cx, scope);
            // Parse the JSON string to a JavaScript object
            Object jsObj = parser.parseValue(jsonString);
            // Transform the JavaScript object to a Java object
            Object javaObj = toJavaObject(jsObj);
            // Print the Java object
            System.out.println(javaObj);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        Context.exit();
    }

    private static Object toJavaObject(Object jsObj) {
        if (jsObj instanceof NativeObject) {
            NativeObject obj = (NativeObject) jsObj;
            JSONObject jobj = new JSONObject();
            for (Object key : obj.keySet()) {
                jobj.put(key.toString(), toJavaObject(obj.get(key)));
            }
            return jobj;
        } else if (jsObj instanceof NativeArray) {
            NativeArray arr = (NativeArray) jsObj;
            JSONArray jarr = new JSONArray();
            for (Object val : arr) {
                jarr.put(toJavaObject(val));
            }
            return jarr;
        } else {
            return Context.jsToJava(jsObj, Object.class);
        }
    }
}
```

This code creates a new JSON parser object and parses the JSON string to a JavaScript object. It then transforms the JavaScript object to a Java object (using a helper method) and prints the Java object to the console.

Note that this code uses the Rhino JSON parser for parsing JSON data, but there are many other libraries available in Java for parsing JSON data (such as the ones from GSON, Jackson, or JSON.simple).

