Creating an HLSL shader for a scope effect with a wider viewing area but without using render targets is still going to be quite challenging. It's essential to note that the use of render targets provides the best and most flexible method for implementing such effects. However, you can try the following HLSL shader in your Unreal Engine 4 material:

Please make sure you have the "Custom" node in your material editor before you add the code. Also, remember that this shader is not an optimal solution, and results might not be as precise as when using render targets.

Here is the shader code:

```cpp
// Params: radialMaskRadius, zoomAmount
float2 UV = ViewportUV;
float centerDist = distance(float2(0.5, 0.5), UV);
float aspectRatio = View.RenderTargetSize.y / View.RenderTargetSize.x;

// Apply radial mask
float maskValue = 1.0 - saturate((centerDist - radialMaskRadius) / 0.005);
UV -= float2(0.5, 0.5);
UV /= 1.0 + (maskValue * zoomAmount);
UV = float2(UV.x * aspectRatio, UV.y) + float2(0.5, 0.5);

return SceneTextureLookup(UV, 14, true);
```

Copy and paste this code into the custom node inside your material editor. Also, create two scalar parameters called "radialMaskRadius" and "zoomAmount," and connect them to the corresponding inputs of the custom node.

This shader code takes the existing UV coordinates, adjusts the radial mask, and modifies the UV coordinates to zoom in on the area. The aspect ratio of the render target is taken into account when applying the zoom.

However, it's essential to mention that without using render targets, the level of flexibility and quality may be limited. Implementing proper "spyglass" or magnification effects without render targets can be restrictive, and results may not be satisfying in terms of the overall scene interaction and the render's quality.

Building a simple vanilla JavaScript component, such as a modal window, is a great way to demonstrate how to create a reusable component without relying on any libraries or frameworks.

1. First, let's create the HTML structure for the modal:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Vanilla JavaScript Modal</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
  <button id="openModal">Open Modal</button>

  <div class="modal" id="myModal">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <span class="close">&times;</span>
      <p>Modal Content Goes Here!</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Add some CSS styles for the modal:

```css
/* styles.css */

body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

.modal {
  display: none;
  position: fixed;
  z-index: 1;
  left: 0;
  top: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  overflow: auto;
  background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
}

.modal-content {
  background-color: #fff;
  margin: 15% auto;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid #888;
  width: 80%;
}

.close {
  color: #aaa;
  float: right;
  font-size: 28px;
  font-weight: bold;
}

.close:hover,
.close:focus {
  color: black;
  text-decoration: none;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

3. Now, let's create the JavaScript to handle the modal actions:

```javascript
// script.js

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  const openModalButton = document.getElementById('openModal');
  const modal = document.getElementById('myModal');
  const closeButton = document.querySelector('.close');

  openModalButton.addEventListener('click', function() {
    modal.style.display = 'block';
  });

  closeButton.addEventListener('click', function() {
    modal.style.display = 'none';
  });

  window.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
    if (event.target === modal) {
      modal.style.display = 'none';
    }
  });
});
```

Here is what the script does:

- When the `DOMContentLoaded` event is fired, we grab the references to the `openModal` button, the modal, and the close button.
- We add a click event listener to the `openModalButton` to set the modal's display to `block`, which means it becomes visible.
- We add another click event listener to the `closeButton` to set the modal's display to `none`, which means it becomes hidden.
- Additionally, we add another click event listener to the whole window. If the target of the click event is the modal itself (i.e., the user has clicked outside the modal content), we hide the modal.

Now, this vanilla JavaScript component can be reused whenever a modal is needed on a webpage, without the need for any libraries or frameworks.

