To create a userscript for twitch.tv that pauses the video player when the "latency to broadcaster" is over 1 second, you can use an extension like Tampermonkey or Greasemonkey. First, install one of these extensions to your browser (Tampermonkey for Chrome or Greasemonkey for Firefox) and then create a new script with the following code:

```
// ==UserScript==
// @name         Twitch Latency Pause
// @namespace    http://tampermonkey.net/
// @version      0.1
// @description  Pauses Twitch player when the latency > 1 second
// @author       You
// @match        https://www.twitch.tv/*
// @grant        none
// ==/UserScript==

(function() {
    'use strict';

    setInterval(() => {
        const latencyElement = document.querySelector('[data-test-selector=Latency]');
        if (latencyElement) {
            const latencyText = latencyElement.innerText;
            const latencyMatch = latencyText.match(/(\d+(\.\d+)?)/);
            if (latencyMatch) {
                const latency = parseFloat(latencyMatch[1]);
                const player = document.querySelector('.video-player__container video');
                if (player) {
                    if (latency > 1 && !player.paused) {
                        player.pause();
                        console.log(`Paused player due to latency: ${latency}s`);
                    } else if (latency <= 1 && player.paused) {
                        player.play();
                        console.log(`Resumed player with latency: ${latency}s`);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }, 1000); // Check every 1 second
})();
```

This script will run on any twitch.tv page and checks the latency to the broadcaster every 1 second. If the latency is more than 1 second, it will pause the video player, and when the latency drops to 1 second or below, it will resume the video player.

