To replace the `Storage::disk($name)` method in Laravel with dynamic S3 data from the database, you'll need to create a custom filesystem driver.

Here are the steps to do that:

1. Create a custom filesystem driver class in your Laravel application. This class should extend the `Illuminate\Filesystem\FilesystemManager` class.

```php
namespace App\CustomFilesystem;

use Illuminate\Filesystem\FilesystemManager;

class CustomFilesystemManager extends FilesystemManager
{
    public function createS3Driver(array $config)
    {
        // Add your authentication logic here to retrieve the S3 details from the database
        $s3 = [
            'driver' => 's3',
            'key' => $s3Auth['key'],
            'secret' => $s3Auth['secret'],
            'region' => $s3Auth['region'],
            'bucket' => $s3Auth['bucket']
        ];

        // return the S3 driver with your dynamic details
        return $this->createS3Driver($s3);
    }
}
```

2. Register the custom driver in the `app.php` configuration file. Locate the `filesystems` array and replace the `driver` key's value with the fully qualified class name of your custom filesystem driver class.

```php
// config/app.php

'filesystems' => [
    'default' => env('FILESYSTEM_DRIVER', 'local'),
    'cloud' => env('FILESYSTEM_CLOUD', 's3'),

    'disks' => [
        // Replace the driver with your custom driver
        'custom-s3' => [
            'driver' => App\CustomFilesystem\CustomFilesystemManager::class,
            'root' => storage_path('app'),
        ],

        // other disk definitions
    ],
],
```

3. Use the custom filesystem as follows:

```php
Storage::disk('custom-s3')->put('example.txt', 'contents');
```

This will use the custom filesystem driver, which retrieves the S3 details from the database at runtime. 

Make sure to adjust the authentication logic in the `createS3Driver` method to your specific needs.

