In order to stop all `Parallel.ForEach` loops when a break condition is met, you can use a `CancellationTokenSource`. Instead of using `break` in the loop, you can cancel the token and use it to stop all remaining tasks.

Here's the edited version of your code using `CancellationTokenSource`:

```csharp
await Task.Run(async () =>
{
    var cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    var po = new ParallelOptions { CancellationToken = cts.Token };

    try
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(accountsFromFile, po, async item =>
        {
            var client = new WolfClient();
            var loggedIn = await client.Login(item.Email, item.Password);

            if (!loggedIn)
            {
                Message senddMessage = await botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(
                            chatId: chatId,
                            text: "فشل تسجيل الدخول على حساب: " + item.Email,
                            cancellationToken: cancellationToken);
                return;
            }

            if (Convert.ToInt32(Times) == 0 || Convert.ToInt32(seconds) == 0)
            {
                await client.GroupMessage(GroupID, text);
            }
            else
            {
                for (int i = 0; i < Convert.ToInt32(Times); i++)
                {
                    var json = System.IO.File.ReadAllText("data.json");
                    MyData accountsFromFiles = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<MyData>(json);

                    if (accountsFromFiles.IsRunning == true)
                    {
                        accountsFromFiles.IsRunning = false;
                        var jsons = JsonConvert.SerializeObject(accountsFromFiles);
                        System.IO.File.WriteAllText("data.json", jsons);
                        cts.Cancel(); // Cancel the Parallel.ForEach loop
                    }

                    po.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();

                    await client.GroupMessage(GroupID, text);

                    int waiting = Convert.ToInt32(seconds) * 1000;

                    Message se = await botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(
                            chatId: chatId,
                            text: "أرسل " + item.Email + " الرسالة بنجاح.",
                            cancellationToken: cancellationToken);

                    Thread.Sleep(waiting);
                }
            }
            await Task.Delay(-1);
        });
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        // Handle the cancellation here if necessary
    }
});
```

In this code, a `CancellationTokenSource` named `cts` is created and its token is passed to `Parallel.ForEach` through `ParallelOptions`. Instead of using `break`, we call `cts.Cancel()` to cancel the token when the break condition is met. Inside the loop, we check for the cancellation request using `po.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`, which throws an `OperationCanceledException` to stop the current task when the token is canceled.

Please note that this approach doesn't guarantee an immediate stop, it depends on how the tasks work. However, it is the recommended way to handle the cancellation of parallel tasks in C#.

