In this example, let's build a simple ComponentManager class using ES6 that manages the lifecycle of components and handles adding, updating, removing, and rendering components on the page.

1. Create an ES6 class called ComponentManager:

```javascript
class ComponentManager {
  constructor() {
    this.components = new Map();
  }

  addComponent(name, component) {
    if (this.components.has(name)) {
      console.warn(`Component with name ${name} already exists.`);
      return;
    }
    this.components.set(name, component);
  }

  removeComponent(name) {
    if (!this.components.has(name)) {
      console.warn(`Component with name ${name} does not exist.`);
      return;
    }
    const component = this.components.get(name);
    component.destroy();
    this.components.delete(name);
  }

  updateComponent(name, data) {
    const component = this.components.get(name);
    if (!component) {
      console.warn(`Component with name ${name} does not exist.`);
      return;
    }
    component.update(data);
  }
}
```

2. Create a base ES6 class called Component to define a basic structure for all components:

```javascript
class Component {
  constructor(element, data) {
    this.element = element;
    this.data = data;
  }

  update(data) {
    this.data = data;
    this.render();
  }

  destroy() {
    this.data = null;
    this.element.remove();
  }

  render() {
    // Perform rendering logic for the component
  }
}
```

3. Create a custom component class that extends the base Component class:

```javascript
class MyComponent extends Component {
  constructor(element, data) {
    super(element, data);
  }

  render() {
    this.element.innerHTML = `
      <h2>${this.data.title}</h2>
      <p>${this.data.content}</p>
    `;
  }
}
```

4. Create an instance of the ComponentManager class and add, update, and remove components:

```javascript
// Create an instance of ComponentManager
const manager = new ComponentManager();

// Add an instance of MyComponent to the manager
const element = document.createElement("div");
const data = { title: "My title", content: "Some content" };
const customComponent = new MyComponent(element, data);
manager.addComponent("myComponent1", customComponent);

// Update a component in the manager
const newData = { title: "New title", content: "New content" };
manager.updateComponent("myComponent1", newData);

// Remove a component from the manager
manager.removeComponent("myComponent1");
```

This code demonstrates a simple ComponentManager along with a base Component class and a custom component MyComponent. The ComponentManager handles the lifecycle (adding, updating, and removing) of components.

