Here's a sample Java code using iText 5 to compare two PDF files and create a new PDF highlighting the differences between them. I have provided comments to each major line/action in the code to help you understand its behavior:

```java
import com.itextpdf.text.Document;
import com.itextpdf.text.DocumentException;
import com.itextpdf.text.Image;
import com.itextpdf.text.pdf.*;

import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.FileOutputStream;
import java.io.IOException;

public class ComparePDFs {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException, DocumentException {
        String pdf1 = "path/to/firstPdfFile.pdf";
        String pdf2 = "path/to/secondPdfFile.pdf";
        String resultPdfPath = "path/to/compareResult.pdf";

        comparePdfFiles(pdf1, pdf2, resultPdfPath);
    }

    public static void comparePdfFiles(String pdf1Path, String pdf2Path, String outputPdfPath) throws IOException, DocumentException {

        PdfReader reader1 = new PdfReader(pdf1Path);
        PdfReader reader2 = new PdfReader(pdf2Path);
        Document document = new Document();
        PdfWriter writer = PdfWriter.getInstance(document, new FileOutputStream(outputPdfPath));

        document.open();

        int numberOfPages1 = reader1.getNumberOfPages();
        int numberOfPages2 = reader2.getNumberOfPages();
        int highestNumberOfPages = Math.max(numberOfPages1, numberOfPages2);

        for (int i = 1; i <= highestNumberOfPages; i++) {

            document.newPage();

            if (i <= numberOfPages1 && i <= numberOfPages2) {
                PdfImportedPage page1 = writer.getImportedPage(reader1, i);
                PdfImportedPage page2 = writer.getImportedPage(reader2, i);

                // Compare pages
                boolean pagesAreEqual = Arrays.equals(PdfReader.getPageContent(i, reader1), PdfReader.getPageContent(i, reader2));

                Image image1 = Image.getInstance(page1);
                Image image2 = Image.getInstance(page2);

                if (!pagesAreEqual) {
                    image1.setBorderColor(BaseColor.RED);
                    image1.setBorderWidth(2);
                    image2.setBorderColor(BaseColor.RED);
                    image2.setBorderWidth(2);
                }

                document.add(image1);
                document.add(image2);
            } else {
                // Add empty page with border color red and text "This page is extra in file x" if the page only exists in one of the files
                PdfPTable table = new PdfPTable(1);
                PdfPCell cell;
                if (i > numberOfPages1) {
                    cell = new PdfPCell(new Phrase("This page is extra in file 2"));
                } else {
                    cell = new PdfPCell(new Phrase("This page is extra in file 1"));
                }

                cell.setBorderColor(BaseColor.RED);
                cell.setBorderWidth(2);
                cell.setPadding(10);
                table.addCell(cell);
                table.setWidthPercentage(100);
                document.add(table);
            }
        }

        document.close();
        reader1.close();
        reader2.close();
    }
}
```

This code extracts images from each of the pages of the two input PDFs and combines them side by side to the output PDF, highlighting the differences with RED borders if any. For the pages that do not exist in one of the files, the output PDF will indicate the extra page and which file it belongs to.

Note that this code does not specifically compare images within each PDF. It assumes that any images or text on each page are part of the overall page render. Comparing images in a more detailed way would require finding the individual images in the PDF and comparing them, which would require a more complex implementation.

You can accomplish this by adding an event listener to the image in JavaScript, and then using `window.location` to navigate to the desired page, passing the data as a URL parameter.

Here's an example:

HTML:
