    <script src="app.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

JavaScript (app.js):
```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
  const imageData = "some-data-to-send";
  const image = document.getElementById("clickable-image");

  image.addEventListener("click", function () {
    window.location.href = "another-page.html?data=" + encodeURIComponent(imageData);
  });
});
```

In this example, we add a click event listener to the image with the ID `clickable-image`. When the image is clicked, the user will be redirected to `another-page.html`, and the data to be sent will be added as a URL parameter `data`. You can change the `imageData` variable in the JavaScript to send any data you want. Remember to replace `your-image-source.jpg` with the actual source of the image you want to use.

With the provided HTML, I will create an example in which, when you click on an image, you will be redirected to a new page that displays information about the car. In this example, I will send the car make, model and year as URL parameters.

Step 1: Update your `carImage` class in your CSS file (style.css) to give each image a cursor-pointer style:

