```

Step 2: Add the JavaScript code to your existing "myFunctions.js" file:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
  const carImages = document.getElementsByClassName("carImage");

  for (const carImage of carImages) {
    carImage.addEventListener("click", function () {
      const carName = carImage.parentElement.nextElementSibling.nextElementSibling.nextElementSibling.nextElementSibling;
      const carYear = carImage.parentElement.nextElementSibling.nextElementSibling.nextElementSibling;
      const carMake = carName.textContent.split(" ")[0];
      const carModel = carName.textContent.split(" ")[1];

      window.location.href = "car-details.html?make=" + encodeURIComponent(carMake) + "&model=" + encodeURIComponent(carModel) + "&year=" + encodeURIComponent(carYear.textContent);
    });
  }
});
```

In this code, we add a click event listener to each image with the class `carImage`. When an image is clicked, we get the car's make, model and year from the table, then redirect the user to the "car-details.html" page with the car information as URL parameters.

To display this information on the "car-details.html" page, you would need to parse the URL parameters on that page and display the car data.

