To add items to a PieChart using LiveCharts in C# WPF, you can either bind the chart to a collection of data or update the existing data series dynamically in code.

Binding the chart to a collection of data is a more flexible approach because it allows you to add and remove items from the collection at runtime and have the chart automatically update to reflect the changes. Here's an example of how you can do this:

1. Define a class to represent a single data item:

```csharp
public class PieChartDataItem
{
    public string Label { get; set; }
    public double Value { get; set; }
}
```

2. Create an observable collection of `PieChartDataItem` objects:

```csharp
public ObservableCollection<PieChartDataItem> ChartData { get; set; } = new ObservableCollection<PieChartDataItem>();
```

3. Bind the chart's `Series` property to this collection:

```xml
<lvc:PieChart Series="{Binding ChartData}">
</lvc:PieChart>
```

4. To add an item to the chart at runtime, simply add a `PieChartDataItem` object to the `ChartData` collection:

```csharp
ChartData.Add(new PieChartDataItem { Label = "New Item", Value = 20 });
```

The chart will automatically update to display the new item.

Alternatively, if you have a fixed number of data series, you can update the `Values` property of the corresponding `PieSeries` object to add or modify items at runtime. Here's an example of how you can do this:

1. Define the `PieSeries` objects for the chart:

```csharp
public SeriesCollection ChartData { get; set; } = new SeriesCollection
{
    new PieSeries
    {
        Title = "Series 1",
        Values = new ChartValues<double> { 10, 20, 30 }
    },
    new PieSeries
    {
        Title = "Series 2",
        Values = new ChartValues<double> { 5, 15, 25 }
    }
};
```

2. Bind the chart's `Series` property to this collection:

```xml
<lvc:PieChart Series="{Binding ChartData}">
</lvc:PieChart>
```

3. To add or modify an item in one of the data series, modify the corresponding `ChartValues` object:

```csharp
// Add a new item to the first series
var series1Values = ChartData[0].Values as ChartValues<double>;
series1Values.Add(40);

// Modify an item in the second series
var series2Values = ChartData[1].Values as ChartValues<double>;
series2Values[1] = 30;
```

The chart will automatically update to display the new or modified items.

To define a global hashset that can be accessed and updated by any function or macro in the project, we can use Rust's lazy static crate. We'll add this to our Cargo.toml in the [dependencies] section:
```
lazy_static = "1.4.0"
```

Next, we'll create a new file in ../project/src called hashset.rs, which will define our global hashset and functions to access and update it:
```rust
use std::collections::HashSet;
use lazy_static::lazy_static;

lazy_static! {
    pub static ref HASHSET: Mutex<HashSet<String>> = Mutex::new(HashSet::new());
}

pub fn add_to_hashset(row: &str) {
    let mut hashset = HASHSET.lock().unwrap();
    hashset.insert(row.to_owned());
}

pub fn get_hashset() -> HashSet<String> {
    let hashset = HASHSET.lock().unwrap();
    hashset.clone()
}
```

Our main function in ../project/src/main.rs can now use these functions to update or read the global hashset:
```rust
mod hashset;

fn main() {
    hashset::add_to_hashset("row1\tcell1\tcell2");
    hashset::add_to_hashset("row2\tcell1\tcell2\tcell3");

    let hashset = hashset::get_hashset();
    for row in hashset {
        println!("{}", row);
    }
}
```

Our subproject in ../subproject/src/lib.rs can also use these functions:
```rust
use project::hashset;

pub fn do_something() {
    hashset::add_to_hashset("subproject row\tsubproject cell");

    let hashset = hashset::get_hashset();
    for row in hashset {
        println!("From subproject: {}", row);
    }
}
``` 

In order for our subproject to know about our project, we'll need to add this to our subproject's Cargo.toml:
```
[dependencies]
project = { path = "../project" }
``` 

Now when we run our main function, we should see the following output:
```
row2    cell1   cell2   cell3
row1    cell1   cell2
subproject row  subproject cell
```

To define a global hashset that can be accessed and updated by any function or macro in the project using only methods from the std library, we can use Rust's interior mutability features. We'll add a new file to ../project/src called hashset.rs, which will define our global hashset and functions to access and update it:
