To create a hashset that will be available in all subprojects of the project from which the project depends, you can define the hashset in the top-level module of the project and make it a public item by adding the `pub` keyword before its declaration:

```rust
pub use std::collections::HashSet;

pub static MY_HASHSET: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new();
```

This creates a static `HashSet` instance named `MY_HASHSET` which can be accessed from anywhere in the project hierarchy by importing the top-level module with `use`:

```rust
use myproject::MY_HASHSET;

fn main() {
    // Add items to the hashset
    MY_HASHSET.insert(42);
    MY_HASHSET.insert(1337);
}
```

To ensure that the same hashset is used across all subprojects that depend on the main project, you should specify the main project as a dependency in each subproject's `Cargo.toml` file:

```toml
[dependencies]
myproject = { path = "../myproject" }
```

This tells Cargo to use the local project at the specified path as a dependency instead of searching for it on crates.io. When you build the subprojects, Cargo will automatically download and build the main project first, so the `MY_HASHSET` instance will be available to them.

To create a `HashSet` that will be available in all subprojects from which the project depends and avoid circular dependencies, you can define the `HashSet` in a separate crate that is shared between the main project and its subprojects.

Here are the steps to do that:

1. Create a new Rust crate for the shared `HashSet`:

   ```sh
   $ cargo new shared-hashset --lib
   ```

2. Add the `HashSet` to the `lib.rs` file of the `shared-hashset` crate:

   ```rust
   use std::collections::HashSet;

   pub static MY_HASHSET: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new();
   ```

3. Add the `shared-hashset` crate as a dependency to the main project's `Cargo.toml` file:

   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   shared-hashset = { path = "../shared-hashset" }
   ```

4. Import the `MY_HASHSET` from the `shared-hashset` crate in the main project's code:

   ```rust
   use shared_hashset::MY_HASHSET;

   fn main() {
       // use the MY_HASHSET here
   }
   ```

5. Add the `shared-hashset` crate as a dependency to the subprojects' `Cargo.toml` files:

   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   shared-hashset = { path = "../../shared-hashset" }
   ```

6. Import the `MY_HASHSET` from the `shared-hashset` crate in the subproject's code:

   ```rust
   use shared_hashset::MY_HASHSET;

   fn main() {
       // use the MY_HASHSET here
   }
   ```

By creating a separate crate for the shared `HashSet`, you avoid circular dependencies among the subprojects and the main project. All the projects depend on the shared crate, but the shared crate doesn't depend on any of them.

