There isn't really a straightforward way to share a `HashSet` among multiple sub-projects without creating a circular dependency. However, you can achieve this using an inner shared library or defining a common abstraction.

Here's a suggestion:

1. Create a separate shared library within the workspace where you define the shared `HashSet`.
2. Add the shared library as a dependency in your sub-projects.
3. Expose functions to interact with the `HashSet` in the shared library.

Now, let's see an example.

Workspace Cargo.toml:
```toml
[workspace]
members = [
    "shared",
    "subproj_a",
    "subproj_b",
]
```

shared/Cargo.toml:
```toml
[package]
name = "shared"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2018"
```

shared/src/lib.rs:
```rust
use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

pub struct SharedHashSet(Arc<Mutex<HashSet<String>>>);

impl SharedHashSet {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        SharedHashSet(Arc::new(Mutex::new(HashSet::new())))
    }

    pub fn insert(&self, item: String) {
        self.0.lock().unwrap().insert(item);
    }

    pub fn remove(&self, item: &str) {
        self.0.lock().unwrap().remove(item);
    }

    pub fn contains(&self, item: &str) -> bool {
        self.0.lock().unwrap().contains(item)
    }
}
```

Then, you can use the `shared` library in your sub-projects:

subproj_a/Cargo.toml:
```toml
[package]
name = "subproj_a"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2018"

[dependencies]
shared = { path = "../shared" }
```

subproj_a/src/lib.rs:
```rust
use shared::SharedHashSet;

pub fn subproj_a_function(shared_set: &SharedHashSet) {
    shared_set.insert("Value from subproj_a".to_string());
}
```

Similarly, update subproj_b to use the shared library.

Now, you can create a shared `HashSet` in your main project and pass it to your sub-projects' functions without causing circular dependencies:

main.rs:
```rust
use shared::SharedHashSet;
use subproj_a;
use subproj_b;

fn main() {
    let shared_set = SharedHashSet::new();

    subproj_a::subproj_a_function(&shared_set);
    subproj_b::subproj_b_function(&shared_set);

    // Access the shared HashSet
    println!("Contains 'Value from subproj_a': {}", shared_set.contains("Value from subproj_a"));
}
```

This way, the shared `HashSet` is available in all sub-projects and you avoid circular dependencies.

