import org.mozilla.javascript.CompilerEnvirons;
import org.mozilla.javascript.Parser;
import org.mozilla.javascript.ast.*;

public class VariableNameParser {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String expr = "var x = 10; var y = x * 2;";
        CompilerEnvirons env = new CompilerEnvirons();
        env.setRecordingComments(true);
        Parser parser = new Parser(env);
        AstRoot ast = parser.parse(expr, null, 1);

        for (Node node : ast) {
            if (node.getType() == Token.VAR) {
                VariableDeclaration varDecl = (VariableDeclaration) node;
                for (VariableInitializer varInit : varDecl.getVariables()) {
                    Name name = (Name) varInit.getTarget();
                    System.out.println(name.getIdentifier());
                }
            } else if (node instanceof Assignment) {
                Assignment assign = (Assignment) node;
                Name name = (Name) assign.getLeft();
                System.out.println(name.getIdentifier());
            } else if (node instanceof Name) {
                Name name = (Name) node;
                System.out.println(name.getIdentifier());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we first create a `CompilerEnvirons` object which is used to configure the parsing environment. We set the `recordingComments` flag to true so that the parser records comments as well. We then create a `Parser` object using the environment and parse the JavaScript expression.

The `AstRoot` object returned by the parser contains the entire AST of the JavaScript expression. We loop through all the nodes in the AST and if we encounter a `VariableDeclaration`, we iterate over all the variables declared in it and extract the variable name using the `Name` object.

If we encounter an `Assignment`, we extract the left-hand side of the assignment using the `Name` object. Finally, if we encounter a `Name` node, we extract the variable name using the `Name` object.

Note that this code only works for simple JavaScript expressions. More complex expressions may require additional handling and parsing logic.

Here is a code snippet that uses Rhino engine to visit all `AstNode` in an `ExpressionStatement`:

```java
import org.mozilla.javascript.CompilerEnvirons;
import org.mozilla.javascript.Parser;
import org.mozilla.javascript.Token;
import org.mozilla.javascript.ast.*;

public class ExpressionStatementVisitor {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String expr = "var x = 10; console.log(x);";
        CompilerEnvirons env = new CompilerEnvirons();
        env.setRecordingComments(true);
        Parser parser = new Parser(env);
        AstRoot ast = parser.parse(expr, null, 1);

        for (Node node : ast) {
            if (node instanceof ExpressionStatement) {
                ExpressionStatement exprStmt = (ExpressionStatement) node;
                AstNode exprNode = exprStmt.getExpression();
                visitAstNode(exprNode);
            }
        }
    }

    private static void visitAstNode(AstNode node) {
        int type = node.getType();
        switch (type) {
            case Token.VAR:
                VarNode varNode = (VarNode) node;
                for (VariableInitializer init : varNode.getVariables()) {
                    visitAstNode(init);
                }
                break;
            case Token.GETPROP:
                PropertyGet propGetNode = (PropertyGet) node;
                visitAstNode(propGetNode.getTarget());
                break;
            case Token.NAME:
                Name nameNode = (Name) node;
                System.out.println("Variable name: " + nameNode.getIdentifier());
                break;
            // Handle other AST nodes as needed
            default:
                // Do nothing
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we first create a `CompilerEnvirons` object, create a `Parser` object, and parse the JavaScript expression just like in the previous answer.

Then we loop through all the `Node` objects in the `AstRoot` object. For each `ExpressionStatement` node, we extract the `AstNode` object representing the expression and pass it to the `visitAstNode()` method.

In the `visitAstNode()` method, we use a `switch` statement to handle different types of `AstNode` objects. In this example, we handle `VarNode`, `PropertyGet`, and `Name` objects. For `VarNode` objects, we loop through all the `VariableInitializer` objects and recursively call `visitAstNode()` on each one. For `PropertyGet` objects, we recursively call `visitAstNode()` on the `AstNode` representing the target object. For `Name` objects, we simply print out the identifier.

You can add additional logic inside the `switch` statement to handle other types of `AstNode` objects as needed.

