    throw std::runtime_error("Failed to allocate staging buffer memory!");
}
```

You create a staging buffer, allocate memory for it, and then bind the memory to the buffer. After that, instead of using this buffer for staging, you create a new staging buffer and allocate memory for it by calling on `BufferUtils::CreateBuffer`. This leads to the same memory being bound to the buffer again, which is an error according to the Vulkan specification.

To fix this, you can remove the first block of code that creates the staging buffer and allocates memory for it, and only use the `BufferUtils::CreateBuffer` method to create and allocate the staging buffer.

