Here's an example Java code that creates a thread that starts at every full hour and runs for 10 minutes every time:

```java
import java.util.Calendar;
import java.util.Timer;
import java.util.TimerTask;

public class ScheduledTask {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Timer timer = new Timer();

        // create a new TimerTask that will run every hour at the beginning of the hour
        TimerTask task = new TimerTask() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                Calendar now = Calendar.getInstance();
                if (now.get(Calendar.MINUTE) == 0) { // check if it's a full hour
                    System.out.println("Starting thread at " + now.getTime());
                    Thread thread = new Thread(new Runnable() {
                        @Override
                        public void run() {
                            System.out.println("Thread started " + now.getTime());
                            try {
                                Thread.sleep(10 * 60 * 1000); // sleep for 10 minutes
                            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                                e.printStackTrace();
                            }
                            System.out.println("Thread stopped " + now.getTime());
                        }
                    });
                    thread.start();
                }
            }
        };

        // schedule the task to run every minute
        timer.scheduleAtFixedRate(task, 0, 60 * 1000);
    }
}
```

This code uses a `Timer` and a `TimerTask` to schedule the thread to run every hour at the beginning of the hour. Inside the `TimerTask`'s `run()` method, we check if the current minute is 0, which means it's a full hour. If it is, we create a new `Thread` and start it. The `Thread` runs for 10 minutes before stopping.

Note that this code runs indefinitely until it is stopped (e.g. by pressing `Ctrl+C` in the console), so you may want to modify the code to include a stopping condition if needed.

