To create a React Native switch, you can use the `Switch` component from the `react-native` library. Here's an example that shows how to create a basic switch with an `onValueChange` handler:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { View, Switch } from 'react-native';

const MySwitch = () => {
  const [isEnabled, setIsEnabled] = useState(false);

  const toggleSwitch = () => {
    setIsEnabled(previousState => !previousState);
  };

  return (
    <View>
      <Switch
        trackColor={{ false: '#767577', true: '#81b0ff' }}
        thumbColor={isEnabled ? '#f5dd4b' : '#f4f3f4'}
        ios_backgroundColor="#3e3e3e"
        onValueChange={toggleSwitch}
        value={isEnabled}
      />
    </View>
  );
};

export default MySwitch;
```

In this example, we use the `useState` hook to keep track of the switch state. We also define a `toggleSwitch` function that is called when the switch is pressed. This function simply sets the state to its opposite value using the `previousState` parameter.

The `Switch` component takes several props:

- `trackColor`: object that defines the switch colors when it's off (`false`) or on (`true`).
- `thumbColor`: color for the switch thumb when it's off (`false`) or on (`true`).
- `ios_backgroundColor`: background color for the switch when running on iOS.
- `onValueChange`: handler function that is called when the switch is pressed.
- `value`: current switch state that is used to determine the switch position.

Tailwind CSS allows you to create custom CSS classes by utilizing its configuration file, `tailwind.config.js`. By utilizing this file, you can group multiple utility classes together under one custom class name. This way, you won't have to write long list of classes in your HTML.

Here's an example of how to create a custom class in `tailwind.config.js`:

```js
module.exports = {
  theme: {
    extend: {},
  },
  variants: {},
  plugins: [],
  corePlugins: {},
  purge: false,

  // Add your custom class configurations here
  plugin: function (context) {
    context.addBase({
      '.my-custom-class': {
        display: 'flex',
        alignItems: 'center',
        justifyContent: 'center',
        backgroundColor: 'red',
        fontSize: '1.5rem',
      },
    });
  },
};
```

Now, you can use `my-custom-class` in your HTML without needing the long list of utility classes.

```html
<div class="my-custom-class">
  Hello, world!
</div>
```

If you still feel that Tailwind is too verbose or not the right fit, you can explore other CSS frameworks such as Bootstrap (https://getbootstrap.com/) or Bulma (https://bulma.io/) which provide pre-built UI components with minimalistic class names.

You can use the "Headless UI" and "React-Datepicker" together with Tailwind CSS to create a customizable date and time picker for your project. Here's how you integrate them in a React project:

1. Install both Headless UI and React-DatePicker:

```bash
npm install @headlessui/react react-datepicker
```

2. Import Tailwind CSS and the required CSS for React-DatePicker in your project:

```javascript
// src/index.js or src/App.js
import 'tailwindcss/tailwind.css';
import 'react-datepicker/dist/react-datepicker.css';
```

3. Implement a custom date and time picker using Headless UI and React-DatePicker:

```jsx
// src/CustomDatePicker.js
import React, { useState, useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import { Transition } from '@headlessui/react';
import DatePicker from 'react-datepicker';
import { format } from 'date-fns';

function CustomDatePicker() {
  const [selectedDate, setSelectedDate] = useState(null);
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);

  const datePickerRef = useRef();

  useEffect(() => {
    const handleClickOutside = (event) => {
      if (datePickerRef.current && !datePickerRef.current.contains(event.target)) {
        setIsOpen(false);
      }
    };

    document.addEventListener('mousedown', handleClickOutside);
    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener('mousedown', handleClickOutside);
    };
  }, [datePickerRef]);

  return (
    <div className="relative w-64" ref={datePickerRef}>
      <input
        type="text"
        readOnly
        value={selectedDate ? format(selectedDate, 'Pp') : ''}
        onClick={() => setIsOpen(!isOpen)}
