To use IModelBinderProvider in an Azure Function, you'll need to create a custom model binder and implement its functionality within the Azure Function. Here's a step-by-step guide to implementing a simple example using an Azure Function with a custom model binder:

1. Create a simple class for the parameter you want to bind.

```
public class MyCustomModel
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
}
```

2. Implement your custom model binder by adding a class that inherits from IModelBinder and implements the required methods.

```csharp
public class MyCustomModelBinder : IModelBinder
{
    public Task BindModelAsync(ModelBindingContext bindingContext)
    {
        if (bindingContext == null)
        {
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(bindingContext));
        }

        var modelName = bindingContext.ModelName;

        // Get the input item from the Azure function context.
        var input = bindingContext.ActionContext.HttpContext.Request.Query[modelName];

        if (input.Count == 0)
        {
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        // Perform your custom binding logic here.
        var model = new MyCustomModel { Name = "CustomName", Age = 30 };

        // Set the result.
        bindingContext.Result = ModelBindingResult.Success(model);
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

3. Implement an IModelBinderProvider to provide the instance of your custom model binder.

```csharp
public class MyCustomModelBinderProvider : IModelBinderProvider
{
    public IModelBinder GetBinder(ModelBinderProviderContext context)
    {
        if (context == null)
        {
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(context));
        }

        if (context.Metadata.ModelType == typeof(MyCustomModel))
        {
            return new MyCustomModelBinder();
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

4. Finally, implement your Azure Function. In Functions v3.x, you'll need to create a custom Startup class for your function app and register the custom model binder provider.

```csharp
[assembly: FunctionsStartup(typeof(MyNamespace.Startup))]

namespace MyNamespace
{
    public class Startup : FunctionsStartup
    {
        public override void Configure(IFunctionsHostBuilder builder)
        {
            builder.Services.AddMvc(options =>
            {
                options.ModelBinderProviders.Insert(0, new MyCustomModelBinderProvider());
            });
        }
    }
}
```

5. Use your custom model in your Azure Function.

```csharp
[FunctionName("Function1")]
public IActionResult Run(
    [HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Function, "get", Route = null)]
    HttpRequest req,
    MyCustomModel model,
    ILogger log)
{
    log.LogInformation("C# HTTP trigger function processed a request.");

    string result = $"Bound model: Name={model.Name}, Age={model.Age}";
    return (ActionResult)new OkObjectResult(result);
}
```

Now, your Azure Function should use your custom model binder when receiving requests. Note that this example is quite simple and may not cover your specific use case, but it should provide a good starting point for implementing custom model binding in an Azure Function.

