1. Efficient Solution using `std::process::Command`

Most efficient way of copying the build result into a string variable will be using `std::process::Command` to run the `cargo build` command and then capture and parse the output.

```rust
use std::process::Command;
use std::str;

fn main() {
    let output = Command::new("cargo")
        .arg("build")
        .output()
        .expect("Failed to execute 'cargo build'");

    let build_results = str::from_utf8(&output.stdout).unwrap();
    println!("Build Results: \n{}", build_results);
}
```

2. Solution using `std::process::Command` and collecting the output into a `Vec<u8>`

This is a similar solution to the first one but using a `Vec<u8>` to store the output.

```rust
