Solution 1: Reading Build Results into a String in Rust using std library

```rust
use std::process::Command;
use std::io::prelude::*;

fn main() {
    let output = Command::new("sh")
                         .arg("-c")
                         .arg("gcc -Wall -o my_program my_program.c")
                         .output()
                         .expect("Failed to execute command");

    let stdout = String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stdout);

    println!("{}", stdout);
}
```

This solution uses the `Command` struct from the std library to execute the `gcc` command with the appropriate flags. The `output()` method is called on the `Command` object to obtain the Build Results. The `from_utf8_lossy()` method is called on the resulting `stdout` to convert it to a `String`.

Solution 2: Using Rust's `std::io` Library to Read Build Results into a String

```rust
use std::process::{Command, Stdio};
use std::io::{BufReader, BufRead};

fn main() {
    let mut child = Command::new("gcc")
        .arg("-Wall")
        .arg("-o")
        .arg("my_program")
        .arg("my_program.c")
        .stdout(Stdio::piped())
        .spawn()
        .expect("failed to execute child");

    let stdout = child
        .stdout
        .take()
        .expect("stdout not captured");

    let reader = BufReader::new(stdout);

    for line in reader.lines() {
        match line {
            Ok(line) => println!("{}", line),
            Err(_) => break,
        }
    }
