Here is an example of strcpy() function with a specified length:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char src[20] = "Hello World!";
    char dest[20];

    // Copy first 5 characters of src to dest
    strncpy(dest, src, 5);

    printf("Source String: %s\n", src);
    printf("Destination String: %s\n", dest);

    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Source String: Hello World!
Destination String: Hello
```

In this example, we are copying the first 5 characters of the `src` string to the `dest` string using `strncpy()` function. The first argument of this function is the destination string, second argument is the source string, and the third argument is the number of characters to be copied. 

Note that when using `strncpy()`, it is important to ensure that the destination string is large enough to accommodate the length specified in the function call. Otherwise, the resulting string may not be null-terminated.

Here is an example of using wsprintf to copy a string with a specified length in C++:

```c++
#include <stdio.h>
#include <windows.h>

int main() {
    WCHAR src[20] = L"Hello World!";
    WCHAR dest[20];

    // Copy first 5 characters of src to dest
    wsprintf(dest, L"%.*s", 5, src);

    wprintf(L"Source String: %s\n", src);
    wprintf(L"Destination String: %s\n", dest);

    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Source String: Hello World!
Destination String: Hello
```

In this example, we are copying the first 5 characters of the `src` string to the `dest` string using the `wsprintf()` function. We are specifying the length using the `*` operator and `%.*s` format specifier, where `*` is replaced by the length as an additional argument to the function call. 

Note that `wsprintf()` is a Windows-specific function and is not available on non-Windows platforms.

There are several natural language processing (NLP) libraries available for C/C++ which can be used to create a struct or class. One such library is the Stanford NLP library, which provides a toolkit for basic NLP tasks such as tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, and dependency parsing.

Here is an example of how you could create a class using the Stanford NLP library to perform part-of-speech tagging:

```c++
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <iostream>
#include "stanford-postagger-4.2.0\stanford-postagger.h"

class NLP {
    public:
        NLP() {
            std::string modelPath = "stanford-postagger-4.2.0/models/english-left3words-distsim.tagger";
            tagger = new StanfordPOSTagger(modelPath);
        }

        ~NLP() {
            delete tagger;
        }

        std::vector<std::string> tag(std::string sentence) {
            std::vector<std::string> tags;

            std::vector<std::string> tokens;
            std::istringstream iss(sentence);
            std::string token;
            while (iss >> token) {
                tokens.push_back(token);
            }

            tags = tagger->tag(tokens);

            return tags;
        }

    private:
        StanfordPOSTagger *tagger;
};

int main() {
