The error you're encountering is due to the temporary buffer created in `GameObject::Render` method being destroyed before the command buffer has finished using it. You should not destroy the buffer and free its memory immediately after submitting the draw commands, instead, you should manage the buffer lifetimes properly.

Here is a possible solution for managing the buffer lifetime:

1. Instead of creating a temporary buffer for each object in each frame, create a "dynamic uniform buffer" that has enough space to store MVP data of all objects in the scene in a single frame. You can create this buffer at the scene initialization or renderer initialization stage.
2. Update the dynamic uniform buffer with the MVP data of each object in the `GameObject::Render` method before drawing the object.
3. Use a proper dynamic offset when binding the descriptor set so that each object will have their own MVP data in the buffer.
4. Only destroy the dynamic uniform buffer when the renderer or the scene is being shut down.

Here are the necessary code changes in different parts of your codebase:

In `Renderer.h`, add a function to get dynamic uniform buffer and dynamic uniform buffer memory:

```cpp
VkBuffer* GetDynamicUniformBuffer();
VkDeviceMemory* GetDynamicUniformBufferMemory();
```

In `Renderer.cpp`, implement the function `GetDynamicUniformBuffer()` and `GetDynamicUniformBufferMemory()`. You can store the dynamic uniform buffer and its memory as class members:

```cpp
	VkBuffer dynamicUniformBuffer;
	VkDeviceMemory dynamicUniformBufferMemory;

	// ...

	VkBuffer* Renderer::GetDynamicUniformBuffer() {
		return &dynamicUniformBuffer;
	}

	VkDeviceMemory* Renderer::GetDynamicUniformBufferMemory() {
		return &dynamicUniformBufferMemory;
	}
```

Replace the line where you create the single MVP buffer and used it in the `GameObject::Render` method with the dynamic uniform buffer provided by the renderer, and update its content:

```cpp
	VkBuffer mvpBuffer = *renderer.GetDynamicUniformBuffer();
	VkDeviceMemory mvpBufferMemory = *renderer.GetDynamicUniformBufferMemory();
	uint32_t objectIndex = ... // Get the index of the object in the array of objects.
	uint64_t mvpOffset = objectIndex * sizeof(MVP); // Calculate the offset of the object inside the dynamic uniform buffer.
	size_t bufferSize = sizeof(MVP); // The size of the buffer

	// Update the buffer content
    void* data;
	vkMapMemory(device, mvpBufferMemory, mvpOffset, bufferSize, 0, &data);
	memcpy(data, &mvp, bufferSize);
	vkUnmapMemory(device, mvpBufferMemory);

	// Bind the descriptor set with the correct dynamic offset
	uint32_t dynamicOffset = static_cast<uint32_t>(mvpOffset);
	vkCmdBindDescriptorSets(*renderer.GetCurrentCommandBuffer(), VK_PIPELINE_BIND_POINT_GRAPHICS, material->GetPipelineLayout(), 0, 1, &descriptorSet, 1, &dynamicOffset);

	// ...
```

Lastly, we need to allocate and bind the dynamic uniform buffer to the descriptor set in material initialization. Instead of using `BufferUtils::CreateBuffer` to create a singlebuffer for MVP, use `BufferUtils::CreateBuffer` to initialize `dynamicUniformBuffer` and `dynamicUniformBufferMemory` with enough space to store MVPs of all objects in the scene. Then, bind this dynamic buffer in the descriptor set for each material.

This way, you will not destroy the buffer and free its memory immediately after submitting the draw commands, so the command buffer should complete using the buffer without any issues. Just don't forget to clean up the dynamic uniform buffer when shutting down the renderer or the scene.

