Yes, to override the `onCreateContextMenu()` method in your activity or fragment, you need to do the following:

1. Open the file containing your activity or fragment class.

2. Add the following method inside your class definition:

```kotlin
override fun onCreateContextMenu(menu: ContextMenu?, view: View?, menuInfo: ContextMenu.ContextMenuInfo?) {
    super.onCreateContextMenu(menu, view, menuInfo)
    menuInflater.inflate(R.menu.context_menu, menu)
}
```

The `onCreateContextMenu()` method is a callback that is triggered when the user performs a long press on the table view. The method receives three parameters - a `ContextMenu` object, the view that triggered the context menu, and a `ContextMenuInfo` object.

In the method body, you call the superclass implementation via `super.onCreateContextMenu(menu, view, menuInfo)` to ensure that any other context menu logic defined in the superclass is also invoked.

You then inflate a context menu layout using `menuInflater.inflate(R.menu.context_menu, menu)`. Here, `R.menu.context_menu` is a reference to a layout file that defines the context menu items.

3. Next, you need to define the logic for what happens when the context menu item is clicked. You can do this by implementing the `onContextItemSelected()` method in your activity or fragment.

For example, suppose the context menu layout file `R.menu.context_menu` contains the following items:

```xml
<menu xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:id="@+id/sort_by_name_asc" android:title="Sort by Name (Ascending)" />
    <item android:id="@+id/sort_by_name_desc" android:title="Sort by Name (Descending)" />
