Lincheck is a library for testing concurrent Java code for the correctness of its execution. It doesn't directly check if two functions use the same synchronized key. However, you can test the concurrent correctness of your code even if it uses synchronized methods or blocks.

Below is a working example of two synchronized methods that utilize the same object lock and can be tested with Lincheck:

```java
import org.jetbrains.kotlinx.lincheck.LinChecker;
import org.jetbrains.kotlinx.lincheck.annotations.Operation;
import org.jetbrains.kotlinx.lincheck.strategy.stress.StressCTest;
import org.jetbrains.kotlinx.lincheck.strategy.stress.StressOptions;
import org.jetbrains.kotlinx.lincheck.verifier.VerifierState;

@StressCTest
public class SynchronizedExampleTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinChecker.check(SynchronizedExampleTest.class,
                new StressOptions().iterations(100).invocationsPerIteration(50));
    }

    private SynchronizedExample example = new SynchronizedExample();

    @Operation
    public int function1() {
        return example.function1();
    }

    @Operation
    public int function2() {
        return example.function2_globalLock();
    }
}
```

This is the `SynchronizedExample` class to be tested, which has `function1` and `function2_globalLock` methods that use the `globalLock`:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class SynchronizedExample extends VerifierState {
    private final Object globalLock = new Object();
    private AtomicInteger value = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public synchronized int function1() {
        value.incrementAndGet();
        return value.get();
    }

    public int function2_globalLock() {
        synchronized (globalLock) {
            value.incrementAndGet();
            return value.get();
        }
    }

    @Override
    public Object extractState() {
        return value.get();
    }
}
```

After running the test, you can observe how they behave when executed concurrently. If there's a concurrent issue with synchronization, Lincheck can catch it.

