To find the PID (Process Identifier) of an application by its name in C++, you can use the Windows API function "EnumProcesses". This function retrieves a list of all currently running processes in the system and their corresponding process identifiers.

Here's an example code to find the PID by application name in C++:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <windows.h>
#include <psapi.h>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

int main() {

    // The application name you want to find the process ID for
    string applicationName = "notepad.exe";

    DWORD processes[1024], cbNeeded, cProcesses;

    // Get the list of all currently running processes
    if(!EnumProcesses(processes, sizeof(processes), &cbNeeded)) {
        cout << "Failed to get the process list." << endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Calculate how many processes are running
    cProcesses = cbNeeded / sizeof(DWORD);

    // For each process, get the handle and the process name
    for(unsigned int i=0; i<cProcesses; i++) {
        HANDLE hProcess = OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_INFORMATION | PROCESS_VM_READ, FALSE, processes[i]);
        if(hProcess != NULL) {
            char szProcessName[MAX_PATH] = {0};
            if(GetModuleBaseNameA(hProcess, NULL, szProcessName, sizeof(szProcessName)/sizeof(char))) {
                // If process name matches the application name, print the process ID
                if(applicationName == szProcessName) {
                    cout << "Process ID: " << processes[i] << endl;
                }
            }
            CloseHandle(hProcess);
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code enumerates all currently running processes using EnumProcesses, then for each process it gets the handle to the process using OpenProcess. Then, using GetModuleBaseNameA, it gets the name of the process and compares it to the application name specified. If the process name matches, it prints the process ID.

Note that this code assumes that the application name is a simple executable name without any path information. If the application name includes a path, you will need to modify the code to extract the executable name only.

To find the PID (Process Identifier) of an application by its name using C++, you can use the Windows API function "CreateToolhelp32Snapshot" and some related functions. This function creates a snapshot of the current process list in the system and allows you to iterate through the list.

Here's an example code to find the PID by application name in C++:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <windows.h>
#include <tlhelp32.h>

using namespace std;

int main() {

    // The application name you want to find the process ID for
    string applicationName = "notepad.exe";

    DWORD pid = 0;
    PROCESSENTRY32 processEntry;
    HANDLE processSnapshot = NULL;

    // Create a snapshot of the current process list
    processSnapshot = CreateToolhelp32Snapshot(TH32CS_SNAPPROCESS, 0);
    if(processSnapshot == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) {
        cout << "Failed to create process snapshot." << endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Set the size of the structure before using it
    processEntry.dwSize = sizeof(PROCESSENTRY32);

    // Retrieve information about the first process in the snapshot
    if(!Process32First(processSnapshot, &processEntry)) {
        cout << "Failed to get process information." << endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Iterate through the process list
    do {
        // If process name matches the application name, get the process ID
        if(applicationName == processEntry.szExeFile) {
            pid = processEntry.th32ProcessID;
            break;
        }

    } while(Process32Next(processSnapshot, &processEntry));

    // Clean up the snapshot handle
    CloseHandle(processSnapshot);

    // Print the process ID if found
    if(pid != 0) {
        cout << "Process ID: " << pid << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "Process not found." << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code creates a snapshot of the current process list in the system using CreateToolhelp32Snapshot, then iterates through the list using Process32First and Process32Next. In each iteration, it checks if the process name matches the application name specified. If it does, it gets the process ID and breaks out of the loop. Finally, it prints the process ID if found.

Note that this code assumes that the application name is a simple executable name without any path information. If the application name includes a path, you will need to modify the code to extract the executable name only.

