To sort child documents in a Firestore database, you can use a query to specify the collection and then add a sort criteria to the query. Here's an example:

Suppose you have a collection called "users", which contains documents for each user. Each user document has a subcollection called "tasks", which contains documents for each task assigned to the user.

To retrieve all tasks for a specific user sorted by due date, you can use the following code:

```javascript
// initialize Firestore database
const db = firebase.firestore();

// specify collection and document ID for user
const userRef = db.collection("users").doc("user123");

// query the "tasks" subcollection and sort by "dueDate" field (ascending order)
const tasksQuery = userRef.collection("tasks").orderBy("dueDate", "asc");

// retrieve tasks data
tasksQuery.get()
  .then((querySnapshot) => {
    querySnapshot.forEach((doc) => {
      // print task data
      console.log(doc.id, " => ", doc.data());
    });
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.log("Error getting tasks: ", error);
  });
```

This code retrieves the "tasks" subcollection for the specified user document, sorts the tasks by the "dueDate" field in ascending order, and then retrieves the tasks data using a `get()` method call.

You can also specify a descending order by changing the second argument of the `orderBy()` method to `"desc"`.

