It looks like the issue is coming from the interpolation being calculated incorrectly. You should consider using bilinear interpolation instead. To implement this, you can edit the `GetInterpolatedHeight` function like this:

```csharp
private float GetInterpolatedHeight(int baseXIndex, int baseZIndex, float baseHeight, Vector3 pointPosition, bool fxNegative, bool fzNegative)
{
    int xAddIndex = (int)(pointPosition.x / StepSize);
    int zAddIndex = (int)(pointPosition.z / StepSize);

    float fx = pointPosition.x / StepSize - xAddIndex;
    float fz = pointPosition.z / StepSize - zAddIndex;

    if (fxNegative)
        fx = 1.0f - Mathf.Abs(fx);

    if (fzNegative)
        fz = 1.0f - Mathf.Abs(fz);

    float height00 = baseHeight;
    float height01 = GetHeight(baseXIndex, zAddIndex);
    float height10 = GetHeight(xAddIndex, baseZIndex);
    float height11 = GetHeight(xAddIndex, zAddIndex);

    // Bilinear interpolation
    float height0 = Mathf.Lerp(height00, height10, fx);
    float height1 = Mathf.Lerp(height01, height11, fx);

    return Mathf.Lerp(height0, height1, fz);
}
```

