In the default `HttpClient`, you can't change the `AllowAutoRedirect` property for per request. However, you can create a new `HttpClientHandler` with custom settings for each request:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;

class Program
{
    static async System.Threading.Tasks.Task Main()
    {
        Uri url1 = new Uri("https://example.com/redirect1");
        Uri url2 = new Uri("https://example.com/redirect2");

        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            // Request 1 with AllowAutoRedirect = true.
            using (var requestMessage1 = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, url1))
            {
                using (var clientHandler1 = new HttpClientHandler { AllowAutoRedirect = true })
                using (var invoker1 = new HttpMessageInvoker(clientHandler1))
                {
                    HttpResponseMessage responseMessage1 = await invoker1.SendAsync(requestMessage1, default);
                    Console.WriteLine($"AllowAutoRedirect: {clientHandler1.AllowAutoRedirect}, Response status: {responseMessage1.StatusCode}");

                    // ... Do something with the response, e.g.:
                    // string content1 = await responseMessage1.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                }
            }

            // Request 2 with AllowAutoRedirect = false.
            using (var requestMessage2 = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, url2))
            {
                using (var clientHandler2 = new HttpClientHandler { AllowAutoRedirect = false })
                using (var invoker2 = new HttpMessageInvoker(clientHandler2))
                {
                    HttpResponseMessage responseMessage2 = await invoker2.SendAsync(requestMessage2, default);
                    Console.WriteLine($"AllowAutoRedirect: {clientHandler2.AllowAutoRedirect}, Response status: {responseMessage2.StatusCode}");

                    // ... Do something with the response, e.g.:
                    // string content2 = await responseMessage2.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

With this approach, you can create different `HttpClientHandlers` with different settings for `AllowAutoRedirect` and send requests using those handlers. Note that instantiating a new handler for each request may not be the best solution in terms of performance for high throughput scenarios.

