        // Additional constructor code
    }

    // Other methods
}
```

In the above example, we have defined two secondary constructors for the `DatabaseHelper` class. Each constructor calls the `super` constructor with different parameters.

Note that the default constructor provided by SQLiteOpenHelper requires the database name and version. If you want to specify the database name and version in the constructor of your `DatabaseHelper` class, you will need to pass those parameters to the `super` constructor in each of your secondary constructors.

To use the secondary constructor, you can create an instance of the class by calling the constructor with the appropriate parameters. For example:

```kotlin
val dbHelper = DatabaseHelper(this)
```

This will create a new instance of the `DatabaseHelper` class using the first secondary constructor, passing the current context as a parameter.

If you want to use the second secondary constructor, you would call it like this:

```kotlin
val dbHelper = DatabaseHelper(this, "my_db_name", 2)
```

This would create a new instance of the `DatabaseHelper` class using the second secondary constructor, passing the current context as well as the database name and version as parameters.

Here's a simple Java console application using the executor service to manage the worker threads and Selenium WebDriver to navigate to google.com:

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class SeleniumWorker implements Runnable {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Pass the startup parameter 'users'
        int users = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
            if (args[i].equalsIgnoreCase("users")) {
                users = Integer.parseInt(args[++i]);
            }
        }
        // Create a fixed thread pool
        ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(users);

        for (int i = 0; i < users; i++) {
            executorService.execute(new SeleniumWorker());
        }

        executorService.shutdown();

        try {
            // Wait for the threads to finish
            executorService.awaitTermination(Long.MAX_VALUE, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        System.out.println("All threads finished.");
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "path/to/your/chromedriver.exe");

        WebDriver webDriver = new ChromeDriver();
        // Navigate to google.com
        webDriver.get("https://www.google.com");

        System.out.println("Thread " + Thread.currentThread().getId() + " Navigated to: " + webDriver.getTitle());

        webDriver.quit();
    }
}
```

Before running this application, make sure you have the corresponding `selenium-java` and `selenium-chrome-driver` Maven dependencies added to your `pom.xml`.

Also, replace the "path/to/your/chromedriver.exe" with the path to your ChromeDriver executable. You can download the ChromeDriver executable from [this link](https://sites.google.com/a/chromium.org/chromedriver/downloads) based on your system.

To run this application, pass the "users" argument followed by an integer like this: `java SeleniumWorker users 5`. The program will then run 5 worker threads, and each will navigate to google.com using the Selenium WebDriver.

