Yes, you can use a different approach to achieve this. The idea is to create a new `HttpClientHandler` per request and set the `AllowAutoRedirect` property accordingly. This will allow you to control auto-redirect per request using separate instances of HttpClientHandler. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;

class Program
{
    static async System.Threading.Tasks.Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        var url1 = "https://httpbin.org/redirect/1";
        var url2 = "https://httpbin.org/get";

        // Redirects allowed
        await SendRequest(url1, allowAutoRedirect: true);
        await SendRequest(url2, allowAutoRedirect: true);

        // Redirects disallowed
        await SendRequest(url1, allowAutoRedirect: false);
        await SendRequest(url2, allowAutoRedirect: false);
    }

    static async System.Threading.Tasks.Task SendRequest(string url, bool allowAutoRedirect)
    {
        using (var handler = new HttpClientHandler { AllowAutoRedirect = allowAutoRedirect })
        using (var httpClient = new HttpClient(handler))
        {
            var response = await httpClient.GetAsync(url);
            Console.WriteLine($"URL: {url} \nAllowAutoRedirect: {allowAutoRedirect} \nStatusCode: {response.StatusCode}\n");
        }
    }
}
```

This code demonstrates how to use different HttpClientHandlers for different requests to control the `AllowAutoRedirect` setting. Note the separate instances of HttpClientHandler with their respective AllowAutoRedirect settings. Just replace the URLs (`url1` and `url2`) with your desired URLs and call `SendRequest()` with the appropriate URL and auto-redirect setting.

Apologies for the confusion. In that case, you can create a custom DelegatingHandler and override the SendAsync method to handle redirects manually. This allows you to control redirects per request while using the same HttpClient instance.

Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    public static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        var url1 = "https://httpbin.org/redirect/1";
        var url2 = "https://httpbin.org/get";

        using (var customHandler = new CustomRedirectHandler())
        using (var httpClient = new HttpClient(customHandler))
        {
            // Redirects allowed
            await SendRequest(httpClient, url1, allowAutoRedirect: true);
            await SendRequest(httpClient, url2, allowAutoRedirect: true);

            // Redirects disallowed
            await SendRequest(httpClient, url1, allowAutoRedirect: false);
            await SendRequest(httpClient, url2, allowAutoRedirect: false);
        }
    }

    private static async Task SendRequest(HttpClient httpClient, string url, bool allowAutoRedirect)
    {
        HttpRequestMessage request = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, url);
        request.Properties.Add("allowAutoRedirect", allowAutoRedirect);
        HttpResponseMessage response = await httpClient.SendAsync(request);
        Console.WriteLine($"URL: {url} \nAllowAutoRedirect: {allowAutoRedirect} \nStatusCode: {response.StatusCode}\n");
    }
}

public class CustomRedirectHandler : DelegatingHandler
{
    public CustomRedirectHandler() : base(new HttpClientHandler()) { }

    protected override async Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync(HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        int maxRedirects = 10;
        bool allowAutoRedirect = request.Properties.ContainsKey("allowAutoRedirect") && (bool)request.Properties["allowAutoRedirect"];

        HttpResponseMessage response = await base.SendAsync(request, cancellationToken);

        while (allowAutoRedirect && (int)response.StatusCode >= 300 && (int)response.StatusCode < 400 && maxRedirects > 0)
        {
            if (!response.Headers.Location.IsAbsoluteUri)
            {
                request.RequestUri = new Uri(request.RequestUri, response.Headers.Location);
            }
            else
            {
                request.RequestUri = response.Headers.Location;
            }

            --maxRedirects;
            response = await base.SendAsync(request, cancellationToken);
        }

        return response;
    }
}
```

In this example, the SendRequest method is modified to utilize the same HttpClient instance. The custom DelegatingHandler, CustomRedirectHandler, is created to manually handle redirects based on the allowAutoRedirect property added to each HttpRequestMessage. This approach enables you to control auto-redirect per request using the same HttpClient instance.

