To cluster together the CPUs of an Android device and a desktop PC for higher compute parallel power, follow these meticulously detailed steps:

1. Ensure both devices are connected to the same network

Ensure that your Android device and desktop PC are connected to the same Wi-Fi network. This will allow them to communicate and transfer data easily without any additional setup.

2. Set up a server on the desktop PC

In your desktop PC, create a Java project to act as a server. The server will be responsible for collecting tasks from the Android device and distributing them to the appropriate resources for parallel processing.

To create the server, you can use Java's ServerSocket and Socket libraries. Below is a basic example of a server:

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.ServerSocket;
import java.net.Socket;

public class Server {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int port = 1234;

        try (ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(port)) {
            System.out.println("Server is listening on port " + port);
            while (true) {
                Socket socket = serverSocket.accept();
                System.out.println("New client connected");
                new ClientHandler(socket).start();
            }
        } catch (IOException ex) {
            System.out.println("Server exception: " + ex.getMessage());
            ex.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

3. Set up a client on the Android device

On the Android device, create a Java project to act as a client. This client will send tasks to the server for processing and receive the results when they are finished.

To create the client, you can use Java's Socket library, as shown in this basic example:

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
import java.io.ObjectOutputStream;
import java.net.Socket;

public class Client {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String hostname = "192.168.1.2"; // Replace with the server's IP address
        int port = 1234;

        try (Socket socket = new Socket(hostname, port)) {
            ObjectOutputStream outputStream = new ObjectOutputStream(socket.getOutputStream());
            ObjectInputStream inputStream = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());

            // Send task for processing
            Task taskToSend = new Task(1, 2);
            outputStream.writeObject(taskToSend);
            outputStream.flush();

            // Receive result
            Task result = (Task) inputStream.readObject();
            System.out.println("Result: " + result.getResult());
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException ex) {
            System.out.println("Client exception: " + ex.getMessage());
            ex.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

4. Create a TaskManager

Create a TaskManager class in both your PC and Android projects. This class will be responsible for splitting and assigning tasks to different threads for parallel processing. 

5. Implement parallel processing using Java ExecutorService or ForkJoinPool

On both the Android device and the desktop PC, use Java's ExecutorService or ForkJoinPool libraries to process tasks in parallel. Below is an example of using ExecutorService:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;

public class TaskManager {
    private final ExecutorService executorService;

    public TaskManager(int numberOfThreads) {
        executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(numberOfThreads);
    }

    public List<Future<Task>> executeTasks(List<Task> tasks) {
        List<Future<Task>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Task task : tasks) {
            Future<Task> future = executorService.submit(task);
            futures.add(future);
        }
        return futures;
    }
}
```

6. Serialize and deserialize tasks

When sending tasks between the Android device and the desktop PC, you will need to serialize and deserialize objects. You can use Java ObjectStreams to do this. In the above client and server examples, we already used ObjectOutputStream and ObjectInputStream to send and receive tasks.

Make sure your Task class implements Serializable interface.

```java
import java.io.Serializable;

public class Task implements Callable<Task>, Serializable {
    // Your task implementation
}
```

7. Send and receive tasks between Android device and desktop PC

When you have tasks to process, use the Android client to send them to the desktop PC. The desktop PC will process the tasks using its own TaskManager and ExecutorService or ForkJoinPool. The server will then send the processed results back to the Android device.

With these steps, you should be able to cluster together two devices, i.e., the CPUs of an Android device and a desktop PC for higher compute parallel power. Note that this is a basic example; in a real-world scenario, you may need to handle issues such as network failures, security, and efficient synchronization between server and client.

