Sure! Here's an explanation of the regular expression used in the code:

`/.{1,500}([\s]|$)/g`

- `.{1,500}` matches any character (except for newline) between 1 and 500 times (inclusive). This creates a substring of up to 500 characters.
- `([\s]|$)` group matches any whitespace character (`\s`, including spaces, tabs, and newlines) or the end of the string (`$`). This ensures that the substring ends on a whitespace character or at the end of the string. This way, we can make sure that no words are split in half.

The `/g` at the end of the expression stands for "global matching", which means that it will match all occurrences of the pattern, rather than just the first one.

