Setting up a backend server using Node.js for your React Native application involves several steps. Here is a general outline of the process:

1. Install Node.js on your computer. You can download the latest version of Node.js from their official website. 
2. Create a new Node.js project for your backend server using a package manager like NPM or Yarn. Open the terminal on your local machine and run the following command: 

   `npm init`

  This command will prompt you to enter some basic information about your project, like its name and description. 
3. Install any necessary dependencies for your Node.js backend server. For example, if you're using the Express.js framework, you can run the following command in your terminal:

   `npm install express`

4. Create a new file in your project directory called `server.js`. This file will define the entry point for your Node.js backend server and will contain the code to start your server and listen on a specific port. Here's some example code to get you started:

   ```js
      const express = require('express');
      const app = express();
      const port = 3000;

      app.get('/', (req, res) => {
        res.send('Hello World!');
      });

      app.listen(port, () => {
        console.log(`Server listening on port ${port}`);
      });
   ```

5. Define your API endpoints. You can define routes in your `server.js` file to handle different HTTP requests to your backend server. For example, you can create a route to retrieve a list of products from a database:

   ```js
      app.get('/products', (req, res) => {
        const products = ['Product A', 'Product B', 'Product C'];
        res.json(products);
      });
   ```

