      const Product = mongoose.model('Product', productSchema);

      app.get('/products', async (req, res) => {
        const products = await Product.find({});
        res.json(products);
      });
   ```

7. Test and deploy your backend server. You can test your backend server by running it locally and making requests to your API endpoints. Once you're ready to deploy your backend server, you can use a cloud service like Heroku, AWS, or Google Cloud Platform.

8. Configure your React Native application to communicate with your backend server. In your React Native app, use a package like Axios to make HTTP requests to your API endpoints.

These are the basic steps involved in setting up a backend server using Node.js for your React Native application. Remember, you'll need to adjust the specifics of your implementation based on your specific application requirements.

