Creating an entire Minecraft client that flies is a complex and time-consuming task, it requires knowledge in programming, game development, and game modding. I can't code an entire client for you here, but I can guide you through the necessary steps and tools to create your mod.

1. Familiarize yourself with Minecraft modding:

Before you start creating a mod, you should get familiar with Minecraft modding and the tools and libraries commonly used in it. Some popular options for Java-based Minecraft modding include Minecraft Forge, Fabric, and LiteLoader.

2. Decide on modding platform:

Choose a platform such as Minecraft Forge or Fabric that best suits your development skills and the version of Minecraft you'd like to develop for. 

3. Set up a development environment:

After choosing the modding platform, you'll need to set up a development environment for creating your mod:

- For Minecraft Forge: Follow the Forge documentation on setting up your environment: https://mcforge.readthedocs.io/en/latest/gettingstarted/
- For Fabric: Follow the Fabric documentation on setting up your environment: https://fabricmc.net/wiki/tutorial:setup

4. Write the code for your flying client:

You can start by modifying player's movement to allow flying, here's an example of how you might override player's movement behavior using Forge's EntityJoinWorldEvent:

```java
import net.minecraft.world.entity.player.Player;
import net.minecraftforge.event.entity.EntityJoinWorldEvent;
import net.minecraftforge.eventbus.api.SubscribeEvent;
import net.minecraftforge.fml.common.Mod;

@Mod("your-mod-id")
public class FlyingClient {

    public FlyingClient() {
    }

    @Mod.EventBusSubscriber(bus = Mod.EventBusSubscriber.Bus.FORGE)
    public static class ForgeEvents {
        @SubscribeEvent
        public static void onPlayerJoin(EntityJoinWorldEvent event) {
            if (event.getEntity() instanceof Player) {
                Player player = (Player) event.getEntity();
                player.abilities.mayfly = true; // Allow flying
                player.abilities.flying = true; // Set player flying initially
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This is just a simple way to get started and allow a player to fly upon joining the game world. You'll need to customize and expand your code according to your needs.

5. Build and test your mod:

After writing your mod's code, you'll need to build, package, and test it in your Minecraft game.

- Building with Forge: Run `./gradlew build` in your development environment, this will create a .jar file in the build/libs folder.
- Building with Fabric: Run `./gradlew shadowRemapJar` in your development environment, this will create a .jar file in the build/libs folder.

Finally, move the generated .jar file to your Minecraft mods folder and launch the game with your selected modding platform to test your new flying client.

Keep in mind that abusing a flying client in multiplayer servers may violate the server rules and result in a ban.

Good luck with your modding endeavors, and have fun creating your flying client!

