To convert your C# Windows Forms application into an API, you can use ASP.NET Core Web API, which is a framework for building web APIs on top of the .NET Core framework. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you with the conversion:

1. Install .NET Core SDK: Download and install the .NET Core SDK if you haven't already, from this link: https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download

2. Create a new ASP.NET Core Web API project: Open a terminal (Command Prompt, PowerShell, or any other terminal) and run the following command to create a new Web API project:

```
dotnet new webapi -n MyWebApi
```

Replace `MyWebApi` with the desired project name. After running the command, you'll have a new folder with the project files.

3. Open the project: Navigate to the newly created project folder and open the `.csproj` file in Visual Studio or your preferred C# editor.

4. Move your existing code: From your Windows Forms application, move your logic code (classes, libraries, etc.) into the new Web API project. Depending on your code structure, you may need to adjust namespaces and references accordingly.

5. Create API controllers: In the new Web API project, create controllers that will handle API requests. You can create controllers by adding new classes to the "Controllers" folder and inheriting from the `ControllerBase` class. In these controllers, you'll implement the endpoints of your API and return data as JSON.

For example, a simple API controller could look like this:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace MyWebApi.Controllers
{
    [ApiController]
    [Route("api/[controller]")]
    public class SampleController : ControllerBase
    {
        [HttpGet]
        public ActionResult<IEnumerable<string>> Get()
        {
            // Replace the following line with your logic.
            return new string[] { "value1", "value2" };
        }
    }
}
```

6. Host and test your API: Press F5 to build and run your project. This will start a local web server that hosts your API. You can test your API endpoints using tools like Postman or by navigating to the appropriate URL in your browser.

7. Publish your API: Once you're satisfied with your API's functionality, you can publish it to a web server (IIS, Azure, etc.) or use a platform like Azure Functions to create a serverless API. For more information on publishing, refer to the official documentation: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/host-and-deploy/

If you need further assistance or specific guidance on converting your existing code, please provide more details about your Windows Forms application.

